__d(function(e,a,t,o,n,s,i){n.exports=[{id:"act-001",category:"Action",emoji:"\ud83c\udfe8",title:"Quake in a 9th-floor hotel room",scenario:"You're in a Tokyo hotel room on the 9th floor. Suddenly, strong shaking starts.",question:"What should you do FIRST?",choices:["Run to the elevator and evacuate the building","Protect your head and take cover under a sturdy desk or table","Rush to the balcony to see what's happening outside","Call the front desk and ask what to do"],answer:1,explanation:"During strong shaking, ensure your own safety first: protect your head and take cover under something sturdy. Never use elevators during or right after an earthquake. Modern Japanese buildings are built to strict earthquake-resistance standards; in recent major quakes in Japan, about 30 to 50% of injuries came from furniture that toppled, fell or slid.",source:{name:"Safety tips \u2014 Earthquakes: at your accommodation (JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/emergency/earthquake/e04.html"},difficulty:1,image:{key:"furniture-injuries",caption:"In recent major earthquakes, about 30\u201350% of injuries came from furniture that toppled, fell or moved (Tokyo Fire Department)"},resources:["emergency-numbers"],infoLink:{name:"Tokyo Fire Department \u2014 injuries from toppling furniture (Japanese page)",url:"https://www.tfd.metro.tokyo.lg.jp/lfe/bou_topic/kaguten/danger.html"}},{id:"act-002",category:"Action",emoji:"\ud83e\udea7",title:"An \xd7 under the tsunami symbol",scenario:"After the quake, you reach the park an evacuation sign pointed to. On the sign, a row of small square symbols \u2014 and under the wave labeled \u6d25\u6ce2 Tsunami there is an \xd7.",question:"What is that \xd7 under the wave telling you?",choices:["A tsunami is considered unlikely in this area","This site is NOT for tsunami \u2014 if a tsunami threatens, keep going to higher ground","The tsunami drill is held somewhere else","The sign is damaged and should be reported"],answer:1,explanation:"An evacuation site is designated per disaster, and the row of symbols shows which ones: flood, debris flow, tsunami and storm surge, landslide, large fire. Tsunami and storm surge share one wave symbol, so a sign often shows two waves side by side \u2014 read the label on each box. A \u25cb under a symbol means the site handles that disaster; an \xd7 means it does not \u2014 a riverside park can be the right place in a big fire and the wrong place in a flood or tsunami. The symbols were standardized nationwide in 2016, so they read the same in every town. Glance at them as you arrive: in a tsunami what counts is height, and the place to be is one marked for the wave \u2014 or simply higher ground.",source:{name:"Disaster-type evacuation sign guidebook (JIS Z 9098), on the Cabinet Office site (Japanese page)",url:"https://www.bousai.go.jp/kyoiku/zukigo/pdf/symbol_02.pdf"},difficulty:1,image:{key:"disaster-symbols",caption:"Disaster symbols on an evacuation-area sign",withQuestion:!0},infoLink:{name:"Cabinet Office and FDMA \u2014 notice on standardized evacuation-site signs (Japanese page)",url:"https://www.bousai.go.jp/kyoiku/zukigo/pdf/symbol_03.pdf"}},{id:"act-003",category:"Action",emoji:"\ud83c\udfeb",title:"Safe hotel or evacuation shelter",scenario:"The shaking has stopped. Your hotel is undamaged and the staff say it's safe to stay. Outside, people are walking toward a nearby school.",question:"Should you follow them to the evacuation shelter?",choices:["Yes \u2014 an evacuation shelter is safer than any hotel","No \u2014 if the building you're in is safe, staying there is usually better","Yes \u2014 foreign visitors are given priority at evacuation shelters","Only if you can't understand the Japanese news"],answer:1,explanation:"An evacuation shelter is for people whose building can no longer be lived in. If your accommodation came through undamaged and the staff say to stay, you are better off where you are: you keep a bed, your luggage and a bathroom, and you avoid taking up space in a hall that a city the size of Tokyo already expects to be over capacity. Tokyo\u2019s own guidance is to shelter where you are living whenever that is possible \u2014 a crowded shelter also makes people unwell through the sudden change of environment. Go if the building is damaged, if staff tell you to leave, or if there is a tsunami or fire risk nearby.",source:{name:"Disaster Preparedness Tokyo (Tokyo Metropolitan Government)",url:"https://www.bousai.metro.tokyo.lg.jp/_res/projects/default_project/_page_/001/029/136/tb2023_e_00.pdf"},appLink:{name:"Safety tips \u2014 official alerts app (App Store)",url:"https://apps.apple.com/app/safety-tips/id858357174"},difficulty:1,image:{key:"stay-or-shelter",caption:"After the shaking, check first for danger, then whether you can stay. If both are fine, stay where you are (based on Disaster Preparedness Tokyo)"}},{id:"act-004",category:"Action",emoji:"\ud83c\udf73",title:"Quake while cooking at the stove",scenario:"You're cooking at a guesthouse's shared kitchen when strong shaking begins. A pot is on the gas stove.",question:"What is the safest first move?",choices:["Move away from the stove and protect yourself; deal with the fire after the shaking stops","Stay at the stove and turn off the gas no matter what","Grab the hot pot and carry it to the sink","Throw water toward the stove from a distance"],answer:0,explanation:"Your safety comes first \u2014 hot oil and boiling water are immediate dangers during shaking. In Japan, gas meters with safety devices trip automatically at around seismic intensity 5 Lower or higher, stopping the gas supply. Turn burners off after the shaking stops.",source:{name:"Tokyo Intercultural Portal Site \u2014 In the Event of an Earthquake (Tokyo Metropolitan Foundation TSUNAGARI)",url:"https://tabunka.tokyo-tsunagari.or.jp/english/disaster/howto/event_of_earthquake.html"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"kitchen-quake",caption:"While it shakes, move away from the stove; turn the flame off once it stops. In strong shaking the gas meter cuts the gas by itself"},infoLink:{name:"JMA Seismic Intensity Scale (explanation tables)",url:"https://www.jma.go.jp/jma/en/Activities/inttable.html"}},{id:"act-005",category:"Action",emoji:"\ud83d\udecf\ufe0f",title:"2 a.m. blackout and broken glass",scenario:"It's 2 a.m. The shaking has stopped. The power is out, your room is pitch dark, and you heard something glass break.",question:"What do you do first?",choices:["Feel your way to the door in the dark \u2014 getting out matters more than your feet","Light the floor with your phone and put something on your feet before you stand up","Stay in bed until it gets light outside","Open the curtains and wait for your eyes to adjust"],answer:1,explanation:"After the shaking stops, the floor is where people get hurt \u2014 broken window glass and pieces of light fixtures, stepped on in the dark with bare feet. Japan\u2019s Fire and Disaster Management Agency names this hazard and recommends keeping thick socks or slippers, a flashlight and a radio next to where you sleep. Your phone screen is bright enough. Put something on your feet, then check the room, and keep the light with you: the corridor and the stairwell may be dark too.",source:{name:"FDMA Earthquake Disaster Manual \u2014 while you are asleep (Japanese page)",url:"https://www.fdma.go.jp/relocation/bousai_manual/occ/occurrence112.html"},difficulty:1,infoLink:{name:"Safety tips \u2014 Earthquakes: at your accommodation (JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/emergency/earthquake/e04.html"}},{id:"act-006",category:"Action",emoji:"\ud83c\udffa",title:"Quake among shelves of ceramics",scenario:"You\u2019re on the souvenir floor of a department store when strong shaking starts. Tall shelves of ceramics stand on every side.",question:"Where is the safest place to be right now?",choices:["Under the nearest display table","Somewhere with nothing stacked around it \u2014 an elevator lobby, or beside a pillar","By the entrance, so you can step outside the moment it stops","Against the back wall, out of the middle of the floor"],answer:1,explanation:"In a shop, the first danger is the stock: things that can topple or fall. The advice for shops and department stores in Japan is to move to where there is the least of it: an elevator lobby, a spot with few goods on display, or the side of a pillar. A display table is not a desk \u2014 it is a shelf with things standing on it, and they will fall off. Cover your head with your bag or a shopping basket while you move. Afterwards, watch where you step: ceramics and glassware break and end up on the floor. And when you leave, take the stairs, even if the elevator is still running.",source:{name:"Safety tips \u2014 Earthquakes: at tourist and commercial facilities (JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/emergency/earthquake/e05.html"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"shop-pillar",caption:"In a shop, watch the stock first. Move clear of tall shelves \u2014 beside a pillar, or anywhere with little stacked \u2014 and cover your head as you go"},infoLink:{name:"FDMA Earthquake Disaster Manual \u2014 in a supermarket or department store (Japanese page)",url:"https://www.fdma.go.jp/relocation/bousai_manual/occ/occurrence140.html"}},{id:"act-007",category:"Action",emoji:"\ud83d\uded7",title:"EEW sounds in a hotel elevator",scenario:"You're alone in a hotel elevator when your phone sounds an Earthquake Early Warning.",question:"What should you do?",choices:["Press the buttons for ALL floors and get off at the first one where it stops","Press the button for your original destination and wait","Press the emergency stop button between floors","Sit down inside and wait for the shaking to end"],answer:0,explanation:"Elevators are dangerous in earthquakes. In Japan many stop at the nearest floor on their own when they sense the first tremor, and some also respond to the Earthquake Early Warning. If you're inside when a warning sounds, press every floor button and exit as soon as the doors open, then use the stairs. Depending on the model, a phone may need a setting turned on or may not receive these alerts at all. Install the official Safety tips app (link below), which sends them in several languages, including English.",source:{name:"Safety tips \u2014 Earthquakes: in elevators (JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/emergency/earthquake/e07.html"},appLink:{name:"Safety tips \u2014 official alerts app (App Store)",url:"https://apps.apple.com/app/safety-tips/id858357174"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"elevator-panel",caption:"An elevator panel: the floor buttons, and the yellow emergency call button that reaches someone outside"},infoLink:{name:"Gov-Online (Government of Japan) \u2014 Earthquake Early Warnings and Tsunami Warnings (Japanese page)",url:"https://www.gov-online.go.jp/article/201410/entry-10414.html"}},{id:"act-008",category:"Action",emoji:"\ud83e\udea7",title:"Quake on a street of high-rises",scenario:"You\u2019re walking down a street lined with tall buildings when strong shaking starts. Glass windows and signboards hang above you on both sides.",question:"Where should you go?",choices:["Press yourself against the nearest wall","Away from the buildings, toward open ground, covering your head as you go","Into the nearest doorway","Under a parked car"],answer:1,explanation:"The instinct is to hug a wall, but that is the wrong move. When an office building\u2019s window glass breaks and falls, it scatters at 40 to 60 km/h across a wide area \u2014 and a wall puts you directly under it. Outer wall tiles, signboards and neon signs fall off the same way, and a shopping street has more of them than an office district. Cover your head with a bag and put distance between yourself and the buildings; when the shaking stops, move to open ground. If there is no open space nearby, Tokyo's guidance is to get inside a relatively new, earthquake-resistant reinforced-concrete building. A doorway is not shelter either: it is no stronger than the rest of a modern building, and it is still under the glass.",source:{name:"FDMA Earthquake Disaster Manual \u2014 in office and entertainment districts (Japanese page)",url:"https://www.fdma.go.jp/relocation/bousai_manual/occ/occurrence220.html"},difficulty:2,infoLink:{name:"Safety tips \u2014 Earthquakes: in downtown and office areas (JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/emergency/earthquake/e08.html"},image:{key:"safety-area-sign",caption:"A green \"Designated Safety Area\" sign: the open ground to head for when the streets themselves are the danger. The running figure is the same JIS pictogram as an emergency evacuation area"}},{id:"act-009",category:"Action",emoji:"\ud83d\udc5f",title:"Evacuating from the 10th floor",scenario:"After a strong earthquake, hotel staff announce an evacuation. You're on the 10th floor.",question:"How should you go down?",choices:["Take the elevator \u2014 it's faster than 10 flights of stairs","Use the emergency stairs, wearing shoes","Hurry down the stairs barefoot \u2014 putting on shoes wastes time","Gather all your luggage first so you don't lose anything"],answer:1,explanation:"Don't take the elevator after an earthquake \u2014 you can be trapped inside, so use the stairs. Tokyo's multilingual disaster guide advises keeping slippers or shoes close by so you can get out at any time \u2014 put them on before you leave the room.",source:{name:"Tokyo Intercultural Portal Site \u2014 Preparing for Disasters (Tokyo Metropolitan Foundation TSUNAGARI)",url:"https://tabunka.tokyo-tsunagari.or.jp/english/disaster/howto/prepare.html"},difficulty:1,infoLink:{name:"Tokyo Intercultural Portal Site \u2014 In the Event of an Earthquake (Tokyo Metropolitan Foundation TSUNAGARI)",url:"https://tabunka.tokyo-tsunagari.or.jp/english/disaster/howto/event_of_earthquake.html"}},{id:"act-010",category:"Action",emoji:"\ud83d\ude85",title:"Shinkansen brakes for a quake",scenario:"Your Shinkansen suddenly brakes hard, and an announcement mentions an earthquake.",question:"What should you do?",choices:["Hold on to a handrail or seat, stay in place, and follow the crew's instructions","Force the doors open and walk along the tracks to the next station","Rush to the front car to ask the driver what happened","Collect your luggage and stand by the doors"],answer:0,explanation:"Trains in Japan brake automatically when the railway's own seismometers or the Earthquake Early Warning pick up a quake \u2014 a sudden stop is usually that automatic brake, not a fault. Stay in the train, brace yourself, and wait for crew instructions. Tracks are dangerous and other trains may still be moving.",source:{name:"Japan Meteorological Agency \u2014 Earthquakes and Tsunamis",url:"https://www.jma.go.jp/jma/en/Activities/earthquake.html"},difficulty:1},{id:"act-011",category:"Action",emoji:"\ud83d\ude97",title:"EEW while driving a rental car",scenario:"You're driving a rental car on a city road when an Earthquake Early Warning sounds and the car starts to sway.",question:"What should you do?",choices:["Speed up to get out of the area before the main shaking","Brake as hard as you can right where you are","Slow down gradually, pull over to the left side of the road, and wait there until the shaking stops","Abandon the car in the traffic lane and run"],answer:2,explanation:"Avoid sudden braking: turn on your hazard lights, slow down gradually, pull over to the left and wait for the shaking to stop. If you must evacuate on foot afterward, leave the doors unlocked with the key inside so the car can be moved for emergency vehicles. The official Safety tips app (link below) sends Earthquake Early Warnings in several languages, including English.",source:{name:"Tokyo Intercultural Portal Site \u2014 In the Event of an Earthquake (Tokyo Metropolitan Foundation TSUNAGARI)",url:"https://tabunka.tokyo-tsunagari.or.jp/english/disaster/howto/event_of_earthquake.html"},appLink:{name:"Safety tips \u2014 official alerts app (App Store)",url:"https://apps.apple.com/app/safety-tips/id858357174"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"emergency-road-sign",caption:"An Emergency Road sign: after a major earthquake, ordinary cars may not use this road. It is written in Japanese and English, with a catfish on it"},infoLink:{name:"Safety tips \u2014 Earthquakes: while driving a rental car (JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/emergency/earthquake/e12.html"}},{id:"act-012",category:"Action",emoji:"\ud83c\udfad",title:"Theater crowd rushes the exits",scenario:"You\u2019re in a packed theater when strong shaking hits. The lights go out and people start pushing toward the exits.",question:"What should you do?",choices:["Join them \u2014 the sooner you're outside, the safer you are","Stay down between the seats until the shaking stops, then leave only at the pace the staff set","Make for the nearest emergency exit before the crowd reaches it","Use your phone's flashlight to guide people to the doors"],answer:1,explanation:"In a packed room the rush for the doors is a danger of its own. Japan\u2019s Fire and Disaster Management Agency puts it plainly: do not rush the exits or the stairs. And it does not stay dark \u2014 a theater\u2019s exit signs and emergency lighting run on their own supply, and the staff know which exits are actually usable. Cover your head with a bag between the seats until the shaking stops, then walk out when you are told to. The part you can do in advance is to notice where the exits are as you sit down.",source:{name:"FDMA Earthquake Disaster Manual \u2014 in a cinema or theater (Japanese page)",url:"https://www.fdma.go.jp/relocation/bousai_manual/occ/occurrence150.html"},difficulty:2,infoLink:{name:"Safety tips \u2014 Earthquakes: at tourist and commercial facilities (JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/emergency/earthquake/e05.html"}},{id:"act-013",category:"Action",emoji:"\ud83c\udf05",title:"Weak but long shaking by the sea",scenario:"You're strolling along a seaside promenade. The ground begins to sway \u2014 gently, but it goes on for a strangely long time.",question:"The shaking was weak. What should you do?",choices:["Start evacuating to higher ground immediately \u2014 long shaking near the coast is a sign of a tsunami","Ignore it \u2014 weak shaking means no danger","Wait by the sea to see if a warning is issued","Continue your walk but keep your phone in hand"],answer:0,explanation:"Official guidance is explicit: when there is a strong \u2014 or weak but long-lasting \u2014 earthquake near the coast, start evacuating. A slow, long swaying can mean a large, distant earthquake capable of generating a tsunami. Don't wait for the official warning to begin moving.",source:{name:"Safety tips for travelers (Japan Tourism Agency / JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"tsunami-sign",caption:"A seaside sign: 'If an earthquake lasts more than one minute, evacuate to higher ground immediately' \u2014 and it notes this spot is only 4 m above sea level"}},{id:"act-014",category:"Action",emoji:"\ud83c\udd98",title:"Elevator stuck between floors",scenario:"The shaking has stopped, but the elevator you were in stopped too \u2014 between floors, doors shut, and nobody answers when you shout.",question:"What should you do?",choices:["Force the doors open and climb out to the floor below","Press the emergency call button, say you're inside, and wait to be rescued","Push the ceiling hatch open and climb onto the roof of the car","Stay quiet and still to save the air"],answer:1,explanation:"Climbing out of a stopped car is how people fall down the shaft, and the ceiling hatch is locked from the outside anyway. Press the emergency call button, say where you are, and wait \u2014 JNTO\u2019s guidance for visitors says the same. You will not run out of air: the car is not airtight. Rescue can be slow after a big quake because so many elevators stop at the same time, which is why some elevators in Japan now keep a small cabinet inside with water, a blanket and an emergency toilet. Look for it. Many elevators here also have an earthquake sensor that sends them to the nearest floor and opens the doors, so being trapped at all is the exception.",image:{key:"elevator-emergency",caption:"The yellow \u3072\u3058\u3087\u3046 button: hold it down and it connects you to someone outside. The grille beside it carries the voice"},source:{name:"Safety tips \u2014 Earthquakes: in elevators (JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/emergency/earthquake/e07.html"},difficulty:3},{id:"act-015",category:"Action",emoji:"\ud83d\udec1",title:"Quake while in a hotel bath",scenario:"You\u2019re in the bath in your hotel room when strong shaking starts. The floor is wet, and the door is right beside you.",question:"What should you do first?",choices:["Get out and run for the bedroom","Open the door to keep your way out, then stay low and away from the mirror until it stops","Stay in the water \u2014 it will absorb the shaking","Grab a towel so you can leave the moment it stops"],answer:1,explanation:"A hotel bathroom is a small room with one door, and a frame that twists in a quake can jam it shut \u2014 with you inside, naked and without your glasses. Japan\u2019s Fire and Disaster Management Agency says to open the door first and keep your exit clear, then wait for the shaking to stop. The door comes first here because it is within reach; in a large communal bath it would not be, and crossing wet tile in strong shaking is its own danger. Stay away from the mirror, and if the tub has a lid you can hold it over your head. Don\u2019t run for it: wet tile is how people fall. When it stops, put something on your feet before you walk out.",source:{name:"FDMA Earthquake Disaster Manual \u2014 in the toilet or bath (Japanese page)",url:"https://www.fdma.go.jp/relocation/bousai_manual/occ/occurrence113.html"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"hotel-bathroom",caption:"A hotel unit bath: one way out, a big mirror, a wet floor"},infoLink:{name:"Safety tips \u2014 Earthquakes: at your accommodation (JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/emergency/earthquake/e04.html"}},{id:"act-016",category:"Action",emoji:"\ud83c\udfd9\ufe0f",title:"Long, slow sway 30 floors up",scenario:"You're on the 30th floor of a Tokyo high-rise hotel. The building starts swaying \u2014 slowly, widely, and it just keeps going.",question:"What is happening, and what should you do?",choices:["The building is failing \u2014 run down the emergency stairs immediately","It's long-period ground motion \u2014 stay clear of furniture that could topple or slide, and hold on until the swaying stops","It's just strong wind \u2014 tall buildings always sway like this, so carry on","Take the elevator down before the swaying gets any worse"],answer:1,explanation:"Distant large earthquakes can send slow seismic waves that make tall buildings resonate \u2014 this is called long-period ground motion, and upper floors can keep swaying widely for minutes. For the people inside, the bigger danger is not the swaying itself but what can move. Furniture can topple or slide across the floor, and elevators may stop mid-ride. Keep away from anything that can move, hold on to something fixed, and stay off the elevators.",source:{name:"Disaster Preparedness Tokyo (Tokyo Metropolitan Government)",url:"https://www.bousai.metro.tokyo.lg.jp/_res/projects/default_project/_page_/001/029/136/tb2023_e_00.pdf"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"furniture-floors",caption:"A Tokyo survey after the 2011 earthquake: on the 11th floor and up, furniture fell or moved in nearly half of the 36 responses (Tokyo Fire Department)"}},{id:"act-017",category:"Action",emoji:"\ud83d\udfe9",title:"Two green signs, two directions",scenario:"Fires have broken out after the quake and an officer waves you off the block. Two green signs point different ways: a person running toward a circle, and a person inside a building.",question:"Which sign do you follow right now?",choices:["The running figure \u2014 an emergency evacuation area, open ground where you go while the danger is live","The building \u2014 an evacuation shelter, where people stay after a disaster","Either one \u2014 they mean the same place","Neither \u2014 those signs are for residents, not travelers"],answer:0,explanation:"Two different signs with two different jobs. The running figure marks an emergency evacuation area: open ground where you go to survive the danger of the moment \u2014 a spreading fire, a collapsing block. The building figure marks an evacuation shelter: a school or community center where people live for a while after their homes are lost. The shelter is not necessarily open or away from the hazard, so while the danger is live you follow the running figure; the building sign matters later, if you cannot go back to where you were staying. Travelers can use both \u2014 and in Tokyo, people stranded away from home also have temporary shelters of their own.",source:{name:"Disaster Preparedness Tokyo (Tokyo Metropolitan Government)",url:"https://www.bousai.metro.tokyo.lg.jp/_res/projects/default_project/_page_/001/029/136/tb2023_e_00.pdf"},difficulty:1,image:{key:"evacuation-area-building",caption:"Emergency evacuation area (left) and evacuation shelter (right) \u2014 the JIS pictograms",withQuestion:!0}},{id:"act-018",category:"Action",emoji:"\ud83d\udecd\ufe0f",title:"Quake in an underground mall",scenario:"Strong shaking hits while you're in an underground shopping mall in Osaka. Someone shouts that being underground is dangerous and sprints for the stairs.",question:"Should you follow?",choices:["Yes \u2014 underground spaces collapse easily, so get above ground at any cost","Yes \u2014 run for the nearest exit before the crowds block the stairs","No \u2014 protect your head where you are. Running for the stairs mid-shaking is the riskier move. When it stops, leave with the staff","No \u2014 go deeper underground, where the shaking is weaker"],answer:2,explanation:"JNTO's Safety tips adds a condition: \"if there are no fire risks, undergrounds are relatively safe.\" Underground structures move with the ground, so the shaking is not amplified the way it is in a building. But Japan's Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism calls \"underground is safe\" a myth: many malls are decades old, and ceiling panels can still fall. Even so, running for the stairs mid-shaking is the riskier move. The real hazards are falling merchandise, a blackout, fire, and a crowd crushing onto the stairs. Once the shaking stops, don't linger: walk out along the walls, following the emergency lights and staff. \u26a0\ufe0f Heavy rain is a different story: in a flood, underground spaces become very high-risk places.",image:{key:"osaka-underground-mall",caption:"An underground mall in Umeda, Osaka, with lights and signs on the ceiling. While it shakes, stay put and protect your head"},source:{name:"Safety tips \u2014 Earthquakes: in an underground space (JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/emergency/earthquake/e06.html"},difficulty:2},{id:"bas-001",category:"Basics",emoji:"\ud83c\udfd6\ufe0f",title:"Long, strong quake on the coast",scenario:"You're walking near the seafront in a coastal town. A strong earthquake shakes the ground for a long time.",question:"What should you do as soon as the shaking stops?",choices:["Stay on the beach and watch whether a tsunami actually comes","Move immediately to higher ground, away from the coast","Go back to your hotel to collect your belongings first","Wait for the water to recede \u2014 that's the signal to start running"],answer:1,explanation:"A strong or long earthquake near the coast is itself a tsunami warning. Move to higher ground immediately \u2014 don't wait to see the sea change. The Japan Meteorological Agency issues tsunami warnings within minutes, but the first wave can arrive even faster, and it is not always the largest.",source:{name:"Japan Meteorological Agency \u2014 Tsunami Warning/Advisory and Tsunami Information",url:"https://www.data.jma.go.jp/eqev/data/en/guide/tsunamiinfo.html"},difficulty:1,image:{key:"tsunami-evacuation",caption:"Tsunami Evacuation Area (left) and Tsunami Evacuation Building (right) \u2014 look for these signs near the coast"},infoLink:{name:"Safety tips \u2014 Earthquakes: at the waterfront (JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/emergency/earthquake/e09.html"}},{id:"bas-002",category:"Basics",emoji:"\ud83d\uddde\ufe0f",title:"Magnitude vs. Shindo",scenario:"Japanese news reports an earthquake as 'Magnitude 6.8, maximum seismic intensity 5 Upper'. Your friend asks what the difference is.",question:"What does Japan's seismic intensity (Shindo) scale measure?",choices:["The total energy released by the earthquake","The depth of the earthquake underground","The length of time the shaking lasts","The strength of shaking at each location \u2014 one quake has many intensities but one magnitude"],answer:3,explanation:"Magnitude measures the earthquake itself; the JMA Seismic Intensity (Shindo) scale describes how strongly the ground actually shook at each place. That's why Japanese alerts report intensity per region \u2014 it tells you what people there experienced.",source:{name:"Japan Meteorological Agency \u2014 Earthquakes and Tsunamis",url:"https://www.jma.go.jp/jma/en/Activities/earthquake.html"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"magnitude-shindo",caption:"One quake, one magnitude \u2014 but a Shindo for every place it reaches. The figures are an example"},infoLink:{name:"JMA FAQ \u2014 seismic intensity and magnitude (Japanese page)",url:"https://www.jma.go.jp/jma/kishou/know/faq/faq27.html"}},{id:"bas-003",category:"Basics",emoji:"\ud83d\udcca",title:"Levels of the Shindo scale",scenario:"You see 'Shindo 5+' on a Japanese TV screen after feeling a quake.",question:"How does the JMA seismic intensity scale run?",choices:["From 0 to 7, with levels 5 and 6 each split into Lower and Upper","From 1 to 10, like a school grading scale","From 1 to 12, like the Mercalli scale used in other countries","It's the same as magnitude, so it has no upper limit"],answer:0,explanation:"The JMA scale runs from 0 (too weak to feel) to 7 (the maximum), and levels 5 and 6 are each divided into Lower and Upper \u2014 ten steps in total. 'Shindo 5+' means 5 Upper: strong shaking where unsecured furniture may topple.",source:{name:"JMA Seismic Intensity Scale (explanation tables)",url:"https://www.jma.go.jp/jma/en/Activities/inttable.html"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"shindo-scale",caption:"JMA seismic intensity scale: 10 steps from 0 to 7, with 5 and 6 each split into Lower (\u5f31) and Upper (\u5f37)"}},{id:"bas-004",category:"Basics",emoji:"\u23f1\ufe0f",title:"Time until a Tsunami Warning",scenario:"A strong earthquake just hit while you're in a coastal city. You wonder how quickly official tsunami information comes.",question:"How soon after an earthquake does JMA issue a tsunami warning, if one is needed?",choices:["Within about 30 seconds","About an hour later, after ships confirm the wave","In about 3 minutes","Only after the first wave reaches the coast"],answer:2,explanation:"JMA aims to issue tsunami warnings about three minutes after an earthquake. But near the source a tsunami can arrive even faster \u2014 which is why strong or long shaking near the coast means you evacuate first and check the warning while moving.",source:{name:"Safety tips for travelers (Japan Tourism Agency / JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/"},difficulty:2},{id:"bas-005",category:"Basics",emoji:"\ud83c\udf0a",title:"Returning after the first wave",scenario:"From high ground, you watched a first small tsunami wave arrive and pull back. People around you start walking back toward the beach.",question:"Is it safe to go back?",choices:["Yes \u2014 tsunami are a single wave, and it has passed","No \u2014 tsunami strike repeatedly over a long period, and later waves can be bigger","Yes, as long as the sea looks calm again","Yes, but only within 30 minutes of the first wave"],answer:1,explanation:"A tsunami is a series of waves that can keep arriving for hours, and the first is not necessarily the largest. Stay on high ground until the official warning is lifted, and keep away from the shore until any advisory is lifted too \u2014 never judge safety by how the sea looks.",source:{name:"Safety tips for travelers (Japan Tourism Agency / JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"tsunami-waves",caption:"An example, not a real record: the waves keep coming for hours, and a later one can be higher than the first"}},{id:"bas-006",category:"Basics",emoji:"\ud83c\udfe2",title:"Japan's buildings in a quake",scenario:"Your friend is nervous about visiting Japan: 'Won't buildings just collapse in an earthquake?'",question:"What's the accurate picture of buildings in Japan?",choices:["Most buildings are old and vulnerable \u2014 stay outdoors as much as possible","Only skyscrapers are safe in earthquakes","Buildings are safe only in Tokyo, where the codes are stricter","Most buildings are fairly resistant to earthquakes thanks to strict construction standards"],answer:3,explanation:"Japan enforces strict earthquake-resistance standards, and most buildings are fairly resistant to earthquakes. That's why the standard advice during shaking is to protect yourself inside rather than run out into the street.",source:{name:"Safety tips for travelers (Japan Tourism Agency / JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/"},difficulty:1},{id:"bas-007",category:"Basics",emoji:"\ud83d\udcf3",title:"About Earthquake Early Warning",scenario:"You've heard Japanese phones can 'warn you before an earthquake' and wonder if that's real.",question:"What is the Earthquake Early Warning (EEW) system?",choices:["A system that issues alerts just as an earthquake starts, giving seconds to protect yourself before strong tremors arrive","A prediction service that forecasts earthquakes days in advance","An app that senses shaking using your phone's motion sensor","A siren that sounds only after the shaking has ended"],answer:0,explanation:"Japan cannot predict earthquakes in advance \u2014 but the EEW system, operating since 2007, detects an earthquake the moment it begins and broadcasts alerts that can arrive seconds before the strong shaking does. Those seconds are for protecting yourself, not evacuating.",source:{name:"Japan Meteorological Agency \u2014 Earthquake Early Warnings",url:"https://www.jma.go.jp/jma/en/Activities/eew.html"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"eew-phone",caption:"An Earthquake Early Warning on a phone: \u7dca\u6025\u5730\u9707\u901f\u5831, 'prepare for strong shaking'. Phones with a SIM or eSIM from a supporting carrier sound the alarm at the same time \u2014 an iPhone, depending on its settings, even in silent mode"}},{id:"bas-008",category:"Basics",emoji:"\ud83d\uddfe",title:"Who's behind the quake warnings",scenario:"After a quake, your hotel TV shows official intensity maps and a tsunami forecast.",question:"Which organization monitors earthquakes and issues these warnings in Japan?",choices:["Each hotel's own safety department","The police (dial 110)","The Japan Meteorological Agency (JMA), which monitors earthquakes around the clock","The United Nations disaster office"],answer:2,explanation:"The Japan Meteorological Agency (JMA) runs nationwide seismic monitoring 24/7 and issues Earthquake Early Warnings, intensity reports, and tsunami warnings. Official information you see on TV, radio, and phone alerts originates from JMA.",source:{name:"Japan Meteorological Agency \u2014 Earthquakes and Tsunamis",url:"https://www.jma.go.jp/jma/en/Activities/earthquake.html"},difficulty:1,image:{key:"shindo-network",caption:"Shindo is measured at 4,360 stations \u2014 670 run by JMA, 2,890 by local authorities, 800 by NIED \u2014 and JMA gathers the readings and publishes them"}},{id:"com-001",category:"Communication",emoji:"\ud83d\ude91",title:"Number to call for an ambulance",scenario:"Your travel companion falls down some stairs and is seriously injured. You need an ambulance in Japan.",question:"Which number should you call?",choices:["110","911","119","112"],answer:2,explanation:"In Japan, dial 119 for fire and ambulance (110 is for police). Ambulance service is available 24/7 nationwide. If you're not sure whether it's an emergency, the Japan Visitor Hotline (050-3816-2787) offers 24-hour multilingual support for travelers.",source:{name:"JNTO \u2014 Guide for when you are feeling ill",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/emergency/eng/mi_guide.html"},difficulty:1,resources:["emergency-numbers"]},{id:"com-002",category:"Communication",emoji:"\ud83d\udc6e",title:"Police number after a theft",scenario:"Your bag with your passport is stolen in a station during the post-earthquake confusion.",question:"Which number do you call to reach the police?",choices:["110","119","104","911"],answer:0,explanation:"110 is Japan's nationwide police number for crimes and traffic accidents; 119 is for fire and ambulance. Can't speak Japanese? Don't hang up \u2014 police handle foreign-language 110 calls by bringing an interpreter into a three-way call when needed. For the stolen passport, also contact your embassy or consulate afterward.",source:{name:"JNTO \u2014 Guide for when you are feeling ill",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/emergency/eng/mi_guide.html"},difficulty:1,resources:["emergency-numbers"],image:{key:"emergency-numbers",caption:"110 for police, 119 for fire and ambulance, 118 for anything at sea"}},{id:"com-003",category:"Communication",emoji:"\ud83d\udcde",title:"Multilingual help when unwell",scenario:"You feel unwell after a stressful earthquake day, but you don't speak Japanese and aren't sure it's an emergency.",question:"Which service offers 24/7 multilingual help for visitors to Japan?",choices:["Dial 911 \u2014 it works worldwide","Dial 171","Call your hotel's front desk and hope someone speaks English","The Japan Visitor Hotline: 050-3816-2787"],answer:3,explanation:"JNTO's Japan Visitor Hotline (050-3816-2787) operates 24 hours a day, 365 days a year in English, Chinese, and Korean \u2014 covering illness, accidents, and other emergencies during your trip. Save it in your phone before traveling. And note that 911 does not work in Japan \u2014 the local emergency numbers are 110 and 119.",source:{name:"JNTO \u2014 Guide for when you are feeling ill",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/emergency/eng/mi_guide.html"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"visitor-hotline",caption:"JNTO's Japan Visitor Hotline: 24 hours a day, 365 days a year, in English, Chinese and Korean"},resources:["emergency-numbers"]},{id:"com-004",category:"Communication",emoji:"\ud83d\udcac",title:"171 in a friend's text",scenario:"A major earthquake has struck. Your Japanese friend texts: 'If phones stop working, use 171.'",question:"What is 171?",choices:["The national evacuation hotline","The Disaster Emergency Message Dial \u2014 a voice message board for disasters","The English-language news radio frequency","The number for booking emergency accommodation"],answer:1,explanation:"171 is the Disaster Emergency Message Dial: a voice message board provided when a disaster such as an earthquake occurs. You can record a short message about your safety, and others can play it back by entering the same number. It has to be a Japanese number in the disaster area \u2014 an overseas number will not work \u2014 so agree on one in advance, such as your hotel's. The voice prompts are Japanese-only, so check the English how-to on NTT's site (the source below) before you need it \u2014 and web171, the web version of the board, offers English guides as well.",source:{name:"NTT East \u2014 Disaster Emergency Message Dial (171)",url:"https://www.ntt-east.co.jp/en/saigai/voice171/"},difficulty:2,resources:["emergency-numbers"],image:{key:"dial-171",caption:"Dial 171, then 1 to record or 2 to play, then a phone number in the disaster area"}},{id:"com-005",category:"Communication",emoji:"\u260e\ufe0f",title:"Testing 171 on a normal day",scenario:"You try dialing 171 on a normal day to test it before your trip.",question:"When does the 171 message service actually operate?",choices:["24/7, all year round","Only at night","When a disaster occurs and phone lines to the affected area become hard to get through","Only for Japanese phone contracts"],answer:2,explanation:"171 is activated when a disaster strikes and communication traffic to the affected area surges, making calls hard to connect. When it starts, NTT announces it on TV, radio and its official website \u2014 the 171 page also shows whether the service is currently running. Outside disasters (and scheduled practice days), it isn't in service \u2014 so learn how it works before you need it.",image:{key:"dial-171-days",caption:"Off on an ordinary day; it opens when a disaster jams the lines. The practice days are your chance to try it"},source:{name:"NTT East \u2014 Disaster Emergency Message Dial (171)",url:"https://www.ntt-east.co.jp/en/saigai/voice171/"},difficulty:2,resources:["emergency-numbers"]},{id:"com-006",category:"Communication",emoji:"\ud83d\udcf5",title:"Why calls fail after a disaster",scenario:"Right after a big earthquake, you try to call your family overseas, but calls keep failing.",question:"Why do phone calls often fail after a major disaster?",choices:["Call traffic to and from the affected area surges, congesting the phone network","The government shuts down all phone lines on purpose","Earthquakes destroy all cell towers instantly","International calls are banned during disasters"],answer:0,explanation:"After a disaster, everyone calls at once and communication traffic spikes, making it difficult to get through \u2014 even where equipment is undamaged. Text messages, messaging apps, and message boards like 171 use capacity more efficiently and are more likely to succeed.",image:{key:"call-congestion",caption:"Even where nothing is broken, the line gets crowded. A call holds it open; a text needs only a moment"},source:{name:"NTT East \u2014 Disaster Emergency Message Dial (171)",url:"https://www.ntt-east.co.jp/en/saigai/voice171/"},difficulty:2},{id:"com-007",category:"Communication",emoji:"\u26f4\ufe0f",title:"Number for emergencies at sea",scenario:"During a ferry trip between islands, you witness a boating accident.",question:"Which number reports emergencies at sea in Japan?",choices:["119","110","104","118"],answer:3,explanation:"118 is the Japan Coast Guard's emergency number for reporting incidents and accidents at sea \u2014 in 2019, the first reports of some 1,900 accidents came in through it, most of them from mobile phones. Worried about language? State the location slowly in simple words \u2014 and the 24/7 multilingual Japan Visitor Hotline (050-3816-2787) can help when you can't get through in Japanese. On land, remember 110 (police) and 119 (fire/ambulance).",source:{name:"Japan Coast Guard \u2014 Saving lives",url:"https://www.kaiho.mlit.go.jp/e/mission/saving_lives.html"},difficulty:2,resources:["emergency-numbers"],image:{key:"emergency-numbers",caption:"110 for police, 119 for fire and ambulance, 118 for anything at sea"}},{id:"com-008",category:"Communication",emoji:"\ud83c\udfe5",title:"Finding a hospital for a fever",scenario:"You have a fever and a sore throat \u2014 uncomfortable, but probably not life-threatening. You don't know any local hospitals.",question:"What's the best way to find medical help as a visitor?",choices:["Call 119 and ask for an ambulance anyway","Use JNTO's medical guide or call the Japan Visitor Hotline to find hospitals that support your language","Wait until you're back in your home country","Go to any hospital at random and hope someone speaks English"],answer:1,explanation:"JNTO provides an online guide for ill travelers with a searchable list of medical institutions by area, language, and specialty \u2014 and the 24/7 Japan Visitor Hotline can advise you. Save ambulances (119) for genuine emergencies.",source:{name:"JNTO \u2014 Guide for when you are feeling ill",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/emergency/eng/mi_guide.html"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"where-to-call",caption:"119 for a life-threatening emergency, #7119 when you cannot tell, and JNTO's guide or the Japan Visitor Hotline to find a clinic"},resources:["emergency-numbers"]},{id:"com-009",category:"Communication",emoji:"\ud83d\ude92",title:"Fire next door: what to tell 119",scenario:"There's a fire in the building next door. You dial 119 and the operator answers in Japanese.",question:"What key information should you communicate first?",choices:["Your passport number","Your hotel booking reference","Whether you need FIRE or AMBULANCE, then the location","Your travel insurance details"],answer:2,explanation:"119 covers both fire and ambulance, so operators first need to know which one \u2014 'Kaji' (fire) or 'Ky\u016bky\u016b' (ambulance) \u2014 and where. Speak slowly; more than nine in ten fire dispatch centers in Japan can bring an interpreter into the call. Location details like a nearby landmark help crews find you.",source:{name:"Tokyo Fire Department \u2014 how a 119 call goes (Japanese page)",url:"https://www.tfd.metro.tokyo.lg.jp/lfe/119/119.html"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"call-119-questions",caption:"The order a 119 operator asks in: fire or ambulance first, then the address (Tokyo Fire Department's call example)"},resources:["emergency-numbers"],infoLink:{name:"FDMA \u2014 interpreters on 119 calls (Japanese page)",url:"https://www.fdma.go.jp/mission/enrichment/gaikokujin_syougaisya_torikumi/sanshakan-douji-tsuuyaku.html"}},{id:"com-010",category:"Communication",emoji:"\ud83d\udcf1",title:"Earthquake warnings in English",scenario:"Before your trip, you want earthquake and tsunami warnings delivered to your phone in English.",question:"What's the official way to get them?",choices:["Follow random social media accounts about earthquakes","Nothing exists in English \u2014 you must read Japanese alerts","Buy a dedicated radio at the airport","Install the 'Safety tips' app, supervised by the Japan Tourism Agency"],answer:3,explanation:"Safety tips is the official app for foreign visitors, supervised by the Japan Tourism Agency. It pushes Earthquake Early Warnings, Tsunami Warnings, and severe-weather alerts in English and other languages. Install it before you need it.",source:{name:"Safety tips \u2014 The App \u201cSafety tips\u201d (Japan Tourism Agency / JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/app.html"},appLink:{name:"Safety tips \u2014 official alerts app (App Store)",url:"https://apps.apple.com/app/safety-tips/id858357174"},difficulty:1,resources:["emergency-numbers"],infoLink:{name:"Japan Tourism Agency \u2014 Information on illnesses, injuries, and disasters",url:"https://www.mlit.go.jp/kankocho/en/shisaku/kokusai/traveltojapan.html"}},{id:"com-011",category:"Communication",emoji:"\ud83c\udf0f",title:"Letting family know you're safe",scenario:"Right after a major earthquake, you want to tell your family back home that you're safe \u2014 but voice calls keep failing.",question:"What's the most reliable way to get the message through?",choices:["Keep redialing until a call connects","Send text messages via SMS, messaging apps, or social media \u2014 and use the 171 message board if lines stay congested","Fly home to tell them in person","Wait until tomorrow when lines are free"],answer:1,explanation:"When call traffic surges after a disaster, text-based messages use the network far more efficiently than voice calls and are much more likely to get through. One short 'I'm safe' message beats fifty failed calls. Japan also opens 171, the Disaster Emergency Message Dial \u2014 a phone-based message board whose prompts are Japanese-only \u2014 and web171, its internet version, which comes with English guides and is the easier one for visitors. (Activation is announced on TV, radio and NTT's website.)",source:{name:"NTT East \u2014 Disaster Emergency Message Dial (171)",url:"https://www.ntt-east.co.jp/en/saigai/voice171/"},difficulty:1,image:{key:"message-order",caption:"When calls jam, text first; then web171 on the internet, then 171 by phone"},resources:["emergency-numbers"]},{id:"com-012",category:"Communication",emoji:"\ud83d\udcf6",title:"Data-only eSIM can't call 119",scenario:"Someone collapses right in front of you. You dial 119 \u2014 but the call won't connect: your travel eSIM is data-only.",question:"How do you reach 119?",choices:["Keep redialing \u2014 emergency calls bypass SIM limitations","Ask a passerby, shop staff or your hotel to call \u2014 or use a public payphone, where emergency calls are free","Text 119 \u2014 SMS always works without a voice plan","Email the nearest fire station through its website"],answer:1,explanation:"Most travel SIMs and eSIMs are data-only: they have no voice line, so they cannot call 110, 119 or 118 at all \u2014 no amount of redialing helps. Borrow a phone from someone nearby, or find a green public payphone: lift the receiver, press the red emergency button if there is one, and dial \u2014 emergency calls need no coins or cards. Check whether your plan includes voice calls before you travel.",source:{name:"NTT East \u2014 Public payphone guide (Japanese page)",url:"https://www.ntt-east.co.jp/ptd/contents/mag_public_kind.html"},difficulty:2,resources:["emergency-numbers"],image:{key:"payphone",caption:"A green public payphone: lift the receiver, press the red emergency button if there is one, and dial 110, 119 or 118 \u2014 no coins or cards needed"}},{id:"inf-001",category:"Information",emoji:"\ud83d\udce1",title:"How an EEW reaches you",scenario:"You're wondering how an Earthquake Early Warning would actually reach you while traveling in Japan.",question:"How are Earthquake Early Warnings broadcast?",choices:["Via TV, radio, cell phones and smartphones","Only through loudspeakers on the street","By email, a few hours later","Only through a paid subscription service"],answer:0,explanation:"EEW alerts are broadcast with a special sound on TV, radio, cell phones and smartphones. Depending on the model, a phone may need a setting turned on or may not receive them at all \u2014 the Safety tips app sends them in several languages, including English.",source:{name:"Safety tips for travelers (Japan Tourism Agency / JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/"},appLink:{name:"Safety tips \u2014 official alerts app (App Store)",url:"https://apps.apple.com/app/safety-tips/id858357174"},difficulty:1,image:{key:"eew-channels",caption:"An Earthquake Early Warning comes over TV, radio and phones. Some phones need a setting turned on; the Safety tips app provides warnings in other languages"},infoLink:{name:"Gov-Online (Government of Japan) \u2014 Earthquake Early Warnings and Tsunami Warnings (Japanese page)",url:"https://www.gov-online.go.jp/article/201410/entry-10414.html"}},{id:"inf-002",category:"Information",emoji:"\u26a1",title:"Shaking hits before the warning",scenario:"During a quake near your city, the shaking hit BEFORE your phone alarm sounded. You wonder if the system failed.",question:"Why can strong shaking arrive before the warning?",choices:["Your phone was in silent mode, which blocks all alerts","Close to the epicenter, tremors can arrive before the warning can be transmitted","The warning system only works during business hours","Warnings are only sent to Japanese citizens' phones"],answer:1,explanation:"The EEW system needs a moment to detect the quake and transmit alerts \u2014 areas close to the epicenter may feel strong tremors before the warning arrives. That's a known limitation: never wait for an alarm to act. If the ground shakes, protect yourself immediately.",source:{name:"JMA \u2014 Examples of Response to an Earthquake Early Warning",url:"https://www.jma.go.jp/jma/en/Activities/eew2.html"},difficulty:3},{id:"inf-003",category:"Information",emoji:"\u23f3",title:"Time from EEW to shaking",scenario:"Your phone screams with an Earthquake Early Warning. You wonder how much time you have.",question:"Typically, how long is there between the warning and strong shaking?",choices:["About 10 to 15 minutes","At least 1 hour","Exactly 3 minutes","A matter of seconds \u2014 from a few seconds to a few tens of seconds"],answer:3,explanation:"The gap between an EEW and the arrival of strong tremors is seconds \u2014 sometimes a few tens of seconds, sometimes almost none. Use those seconds to protect your head and get to cover, not to run outside or gather belongings.",source:{name:"JMA \u2014 Examples of Response to an Earthquake Early Warning",url:"https://www.jma.go.jp/jma/en/Activities/eew2.html"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"eew-race",caption:"The bar is how long the S wave takes to reach you; the warning goes out a few seconds after the quake, so your head start is a little shorter"}},{id:"inf-004",category:"Information",emoji:"\ud83c\udf26\ufe0f",title:"JMA's Advisory and Warning tiers",scenario:"Checking the weather during typhoon season, you see the words 'Advisory' and 'Warning' on JMA's site.",question:"How are JMA's alert levels organized?",choices:["There is only one level: Warning","Green, yellow and red, like traffic lights","Advisory, Warning and Emergency Warning in rising severity \u2014 with an Urgent Warning between the last two for heavy rain and floods","Levels 1 to 100, updated hourly"],answer:2,explanation:"JMA's alerts rise in severity from Advisory to Warning to Emergency Warning, and an Emergency Warning signals an exceptional danger of a kind seen only once every few decades. As of 2026, heavy rain, river floods, landslides and storm surge come with their alert-level number \u2014 Level 2 Advisory, Level 3 Warning, Level 4 Urgent Warning, Level 5 Emergency Warning \u2014 and at Level 4 everyone in a dangerous place should evacuate. Wind, waves and snow still use the three steps. Knowing the names and the numbers tells you how fast to act when one appears on TV or your phone.",source:{name:"Japan Meteorological Agency \u2014 Emergency Warning System",url:"https://www.jma.go.jp/jma/en/Emergency_Warning/ew_index.html"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"warning-tiers",caption:"Four steps for heavy rain and floods since May 2026, with Level 5 at the top. Wind and snow have no Urgent Warning"},infoLink:{name:"Safety tips \u2014 When a weather warning is issued (JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/emergency/weather/w01.html"}},{id:"inf-005",category:"Information",emoji:"\ud83c\udf27\ufe0f",title:"Emergency Warning for heavy rain",scenario:"Your phone shows an 'Emergency Warning' (\u7279\u5225\u8b66\u5831) for heavy rain in the region you're visiting.",question:"What does an Emergency Warning signify?",choices:["A catastrophe on a scale seen only once every several decades \u2014 take the best possible action to protect your life immediately","A routine alert issued several times a month","A test of the alert system","Bad weather expected next week"],answer:0,explanation:"Emergency Warnings are reserved for devastating events of a magnitude occurring only once every several decades. If one is issued for your area, the situation is exceptional: act immediately to protect your life, following evacuation information from local authorities.",source:{name:"Safety tips for travelers (Japan Tourism Agency / JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/"},difficulty:3,image:{key:"warning-levels-en",caption:"JMA's alert levels: the Emergency Warning is Level 5, at the top, and the band just below it says to be out by Level 4"}},{id:"inf-006",category:"Information",emoji:"\ud83d\uddfa\ufe0f",title:"Tsunami Warning map on TV",scenario:"After an offshore earthquake, the TV shows a map of Japan's coastline with some segments colored and others not.",question:"How does JMA issue tsunami warnings?",choices:["One single warning for the entire country every time","Only for beaches with lifeguards","Warnings are issued per ocean, not per coast","Per coastal region, based on estimated tsunami heights for each"],answer:3,explanation:"When disastrous waves are expected, JMA issues a Tsunami Warning or Advisory for each coastal region expected to be affected, based on estimated tsunami heights. The colored map shows which stretches of coast must evacuate \u2014 check whether yours is one of them.",source:{name:"JMA \u2014 Tsunami Warnings / Advisories",url:"https://www.data.jma.go.jp/eqev/data/en/tsunami/tsunami_warning.html"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"tsunami-levels",caption:"JMA's three tsunami alerts: Major Tsunami Warning (purple), Tsunami Warning (red), Tsunami Advisory (yellow)"}},{id:"inf-007",category:"Information",emoji:"\ud83d\udcfb",title:"What an EEW tells you",scenario:"A Japanese friend explains that EEW broadcasts on TV show more than just 'an earthquake happened'.",question:"What information does an Earthquake Early Warning include?",choices:["The number of casualties so far","Which areas should brace for strong shaking, and roughly how strong it will be","Which train lines will be suspended tomorrow","The exact buildings that will be damaged"],answer:1,explanation:"The public warning on TV and phones names the areas where strong shaking \u2014 seismic intensity 5 Lower or higher \u2014 is expected, and the areas expecting 4. It does not count down the seconds: predicted arrival times come only in JMA's detailed forecast feed, used by railways, factories and some apps. The warning alone tells you whether the strong shaking is heading your way.",source:{name:"JMA \u2014 what an Earthquake Early Warning contains (Japanese page)",url:"https://www.jma.go.jp/jma/kishou/know/jishin/eew/shikumi/shousai.html"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"eew-contents",caption:"The warning names where shaking is expected and how strong \u2014 not how many seconds you have"}},{id:"inf-008",category:"Information",emoji:"\ud83d\udcf2",title:"Tsunami Warning with no shaking",scenario:"You're in a coastal town. A tsunami warning appears on your phone \u2014 but you never felt any shaking at all.",question:"What should you make of it?",choices:["It must be a false alarm \u2014 ignore it","Tsunami can't happen without local shaking, so stay put","Take it seriously and evacuate \u2014 a tsunami can come even when you felt no shaking","Wait for a second warning to confirm"],answer:2,explanation:"A tsunami can arrive even when you felt no shaking at all. Official guidance: when a warning is issued, ensure your safety \u2014 don't judge the danger yourself; evacuate to elevated ground and stay until warnings are lifted.",source:{name:"JMA, Cabinet Office and FDMA \u2014 leaflet \u201cEarthquake! Tsunami! Evacuate immediately!\u201d",url:"https://www.jma.go.jp/jma/kishou/know/jishin/tsunami_bosai/img/leaflet_tsunami_bosai_en.pdf"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"tsunami-travel-1960",caption:"How long the 1960 Chile tsunami took to cross the Pacific. The contours are hours; Japan lies beyond the 20-hour line, top left"},infoLink:{name:"Safety tips for travelers (Japan Tourism Agency / JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/"}},{id:"inf-009",category:"Information",emoji:"\ud83d\udcfa",title:"Two-tone chime on hotel TV",scenario:"You're watching TV in your hotel room when the program is suddenly interrupted. An urgent two-tone chime sounds, and a map of Japan appears with the words 'Earthquake Early Warning'.",question:"What does this chime mean?",choices:["A test of the TV station's equipment","The hourly time signal on Japanese TV","Strong shaking may arrive within seconds \u2014 protect yourself right now","An earthquake happened somewhere far away and is already over"],answer:2,explanation:"That distinctive chime is the Earthquake Early Warning broadcast, cutting into TV and radio programs nationwide when strong shaking is expected. It means you may have only seconds. Learn the sound before you travel \u2014 recognizing it instantly is what buys you time to take cover.",image:{key:"eew-tv",caption:"\xd7 marks the epicenter, red the area where strong shaking is expected. An example, not a real quake"},source:{name:"Japan Meteorological Agency \u2014 Earthquake Early Warnings",url:"https://www.jma.go.jp/jma/en/Activities/eew.html"},difficulty:1},{id:"inf-010",category:"Information",emoji:"\ud83d\udea8",title:"All phones on the train go off",scenario:"On a quiet train, every phone around you suddenly blasts the same harsh buzzer at full volume \u2014 including phones that seemed to be on silent.",question:"What is happening?",choices:["A marketing push notification campaign","A nationwide test of the alert system \u2014 no action needed","A phone network malfunction","An emergency alert was broadcast to all phones in the area \u2014 danger such as strong shaking is expected where YOU are"],answer:3,explanation:"Japan broadcasts emergency alerts to the mobile phones in an affected area at once \u2014 the harsh buzzer means the warning applies to where you are standing, not somewhere far away. Depending on the model, some phones need a setting turned on or cannot receive these alerts. The Safety tips app adds notifications in several languages, including English. When you hear it, act immediately: protect your head and get to cover.",image:{key:"eew-broadcast",caption:"The mast sends once and the handsets in the area that can receive it light up together. It is addressed to the place, not to you"},source:{name:"Safety tips for travelers (Japan Tourism Agency / JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/"},difficulty:1,audioDemo:{name:"SoftBank \u2014 play the actual alert sound (Japanese page)",url:"https://www.softbank.jp/mobile/service/urgent_news/about/eew/"},infoLink:{name:"Gov-Online (Government of Japan) \u2014 Earthquake Early Warnings and Tsunami Warnings (Japanese page)",url:"https://www.gov-online.go.jp/article/201410/entry-10414.html"}},{id:"inf-011",category:"Information",emoji:"\ud83e\udded",title:"English info after the shaking",scenario:"The shaking has stopped and you're safe. Now you want to understand what happened and whether more danger is coming \u2014 in English.",question:"Where should you get your information?",choices:["Official channels: the Safety tips app's alerts and JMA's website, plus embassy and major news links they provide","Whatever is trending on social media","A group chat of other tourists guessing together","Wait for someone at the hotel to mention it"],answer:0,explanation:"Official sources give you warnings, intensity reports, and tsunami status in English: the Safety tips app pushes JMA alerts, and JMA's website publishes earthquake details. The Safety tips site also links to embassies and news media. After any big quake, check official information before acting on anything you hear.",source:{name:"Safety tips for travelers (Japan Tourism Agency / JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/"},appLink:{name:"Safety tips \u2014 official alerts app (App Store)",url:"https://apps.apple.com/app/safety-tips/id858357174"},difficulty:1,image:{key:"info-sources",caption:"Where to check after a quake: the Safety tips app for alerts, the JMA site for details, NHK WORLD-JAPAN for news in English, and the Safety tips website for its embassy and media links"}},{id:"inf-012",category:"Information",emoji:"\ud83e\udd81",title:"Lion and quake posts go viral",scenario:"An hour after a strong quake, posts are spreading on social media: 'A lion escaped from the zoo!' and 'Authorities say an even bigger quake will hit at 8 p.m. tonight!'",question:"How should you treat these posts?",choices:["Share them quickly so others can prepare","Believe the animal one but not the prediction","Assume they're true if they have many reposts","Don't spread them \u2014 verify against official sources; note that no one can predict an earthquake's exact time"],answer:3,explanation:"False rumors spread fast after disasters in every country \u2014 sensational animal escapes and 'insider' predictions are classic patterns. Earthquakes cannot be predicted for a specific time, so a post claiming an exact schedule can be taken as false. Japan does sometimes issue an official notice, after certain large earthquakes, that a bigger one has become more likely for a set period \u2014 it comes from JMA, names no date or time, and is not a prediction. Check official sources such as JMA and local authorities, and don't repost unverified claims.",source:{name:"JMA \u2014 Can earthquakes be predicted? (Japanese page)",url:"https://www.jma.go.jp/jma/kishou/know/faq/faq24.html"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"rumor-vs-official",caption:"A post that names the date and time of a quake can be taken as false. JMA's extra information names no date or time, and is not a prediction"},infoLink:{name:"JMA \u2014 Nankai Trough Earthquake Extra Information (Japanese page)",url:"https://www.jma.go.jp/jma/kishou/know/faq/faq31.html"}},{id:"inf-013",category:"Information",emoji:"\u2614",title:"Heavy rain warning underground",scenario:"You're browsing an underground shopping street when your phone buzzes: a heavy rain warning. Outside, a sudden torrential downpour has begun.",question:"What should you do?",choices:["Head up to ground level or higher floors \u2014 in flooding, underground is the dangerous place to be","Stay put \u2014 underground, the rain outside can't reach you","It's the perfect place to wait out the rain \u2014 keep shopping","Move to the lowest level, where it's coolest and quietest"],answer:0,explanation:"In heavy rain, water flows easily into underground spaces, and since you can't see outside, it's easy to leave too late. Once flooding starts, climbing stairs against the water pouring down them is very hard. The greatest danger is underground in low-lying spots, and in building basements and semi-basements. Most large underground malls and stations have flood-prevention plans, but equipment that holds back water has its limits. When a heavy rain warning is issued, move up to street level or a higher floor early.",source:{name:"Disaster Preparedness Tokyo (Tokyo Metropolitan Government)",url:"https://www.bousai.metro.tokyo.lg.jp/_res/projects/default_project/_page_/001/029/136/tb2023_e_00.pdf"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"underground-flood",caption:"When the street floods, the water pours down the stairs into underground spaces. At about 30 cm on the street, climbing out gets hard (full-scale model tests)"},infoLink:{name:"MLIT \u2014 flooding in underground spaces (Japanese page)",url:"https://www.mlit.go.jp/river/bousai/main/saigai/jouhou/jieisuibou/bousai-gensai-suibou01.html"}},{id:"inf-014",category:"Information",emoji:"\u26c8\ufe0f",title:"Dark sky and thunder by a river",scenario:"On a muggy afternoon you're walking along a small river in the city. The sky suddenly turns dark, you hear thunder in the distance, and a burst of cold wind hits you.",question:"What do these signs mean?",choices:["A violent downpour may be minutes away \u2014 leave the river and get inside a sturdy building","A typhoon is coming \u2014 but those give days of warning, so keep walking","Just a passing summer breeze \u2014 rain won't start until you feel drops","The storm is only where the clouds are \u2014 if it's dry here, the river won't rise"],answer:0,explanation:"A blackening sky, the sound of thunder, and a sudden cold wind are JMA's textbook signs that a towering storm cloud is approaching \u2014 the engine behind Japan's sudden 'guerrilla' downpours, most common on summer afternoons, when the sun has heated the air near the ground. Rain heavy enough to flood streets can start within minutes, and small urban rivers can surge even if it's dry where you are, fed by rain that fell upstream. Whatever the season, once you see the signs, move away from rivers and low ground and wait inside a sturdy building.",image:{key:"shono-hakuu",caption:"Hiroshige, Sh\u014dno: Driving Rain (c. 1833\u201334): the wind bends the bamboo, and travelers run through the sudden downpour"},source:{name:"JMA \u2014 Protecting yourself from sudden heavy rain, lightning and tornadoes (Japanese page)",url:"https://www.jma.go.jp/jma/kishou/know/tenki_chuui/tenki_chuui_p8.html"},difficulty:2,infoLink:{name:"JMA \u2014 leaflet on sudden heavy rain, lightning and tornadoes (Japanese page)",url:"https://www.jma.go.jp/jma/kishou/books/nowcast/nowcast202603.pdf"}},{id:"sur-001",category:"Survival",emoji:"\ud83c\udfeb",title:"Designated shelter at a school",scenario:"After a major earthquake, signs point to a 'Designated Evacuation Shelter' at a nearby school.",question:"What is a Designated Evacuation Shelter?",choices:["A paid emergency hotel for tourists","A military facility closed to civilians","A shelter exclusively for Japanese citizens","A facility like a school or community center, run by the local government, where people stay until the danger passes"],answer:3,explanation:"Designated Evacuation Shelters are public facilities \u2014 often schools and community centers \u2014 operated by local governments for people whose safety is threatened by a disaster. Visitors can seek help there too; follow the staff's guidance. In Tokyo, people stranded away from home also have temporary shelters of their own.",source:{name:"Safety tips for travelers (Japan Tourism Agency / JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/"},difficulty:1,image:{key:"evacuation-shelter",caption:"Designated Evacuation Shelter \u2014 the JIS Z 8210 pictogram used on signs",withQuestion:!0}},{id:"sur-002",category:"Survival",emoji:"\u26f0\ufe0f",title:"When to leave high ground",scenario:"You evacuated uphill from a tsunami warning two hours ago. Nothing dramatic seems to have happened, and your hotel is by the shore.",question:"When can you go back down?",choices:["Only after the warning is officially canceled","After two hours \u2014 tsunami danger expires quickly","As soon as the street looks dry","Whenever other people start heading back"],answer:0,explanation:"Stay on high ground until warnings are officially canceled. Tsunami strike repeatedly over long periods, and a quiet interval does not mean it is over. Follow official information \u2014 not the crowd, and not how things look. If the warning is downgraded to an advisory, stay out of the water and away from the shore until the advisory is cleared too.",source:{name:"Safety tips for travelers (Japan Tourism Agency / JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"tsunami-stages",caption:"Stay on high ground while the warning stands; even at advisory level, stay out of the water. Go back only once it is lifted"}},{id:"sur-003",category:"Survival",emoji:"\ud83c\udf92",title:"Tsunami Warning, luggage in room",scenario:"A tsunami warning sounds. Your passport, laptop, and luggage are in your beachfront hotel room on the 2nd floor.",question:"What do you do?",choices:["Quickly pack everything \u2014 documents are irreplaceable","Ask hotel staff to fetch your bags first","Leave your belongings and evacuate to high ground immediately","Carry your suitcase along the evacuation route"],answer:2,explanation:"Evacuate immediately \u2014 belongings can be replaced, and passports can be reissued through your embassy. Guidance is clear: don't stop to evaluate or collect things; move to elevated ground at once. Minutes matter with a tsunami.",source:{name:"Safety tips for travelers (Japan Tourism Agency / JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/"},difficulty:1,image:{key:"leave-bags",caption:"Leave the bags and go to high ground at once. Things can be replaced, and a passport can be reissued"}},{id:"sur-004",category:"Survival",emoji:"\ud83e\uddf3",title:"Light prep for a two-week trip",scenario:"You're packing for a two-week trip across Japan and want to be earthquake-ready without carrying much.",question:"What's the most effective lightweight preparation?",choices:["A full camping kit with a tent and a week of food","Install the Safety tips app and save key numbers (119, 110, Japan Visitor Hotline, your embassy) in your phone","Nothing \u2014 preparation is only for residents","A paper map of every evacuation shelter in Japan"],answer:1,explanation:"Some preparation weighs nothing: the official Safety tips app for alerts, plus saved contacts \u2014 119 (ambulance/fire), 110 (police), the 24/7 Japan Visitor Hotline (050-3816-2787), and your embassy. Add comfortable shoes and a portable battery and you have the basics covered.",source:{name:"Japan Tourism Agency / JNTO \u2014 Guide for when you are feeling ill",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/emergency/eng/mi_guide.html"},appLink:{name:"Safety tips \u2014 official alerts app (App Store)",url:"https://apps.apple.com/app/safety-tips/id858357174"},difficulty:1,image:{key:"phone-prep",caption:"Preparation that weighs nothing: save 119, 110, the Japan Visitor Hotline and your embassy, and install the Safety tips app"},resources:["emergency-numbers"],infoLink:{name:"Travel Japan \u2014 Staying Safe in Japan (JNTO)",url:"https://www.japan.travel/en/plan/emergencies/"}},{id:"sur-005",category:"Survival",emoji:"\ud83c\udf03",title:"Calm after the main quake",scenario:"A powerful earthquake struck this morning. By evening, things feel calm and you assume it's over.",question:"Is the danger over after the main quake?",choices:["Yes \u2014 earthquakes never repeat in the same area","Yes, as long as your building survived the first one","Mostly \u2014 only tiny tremors can follow","Not necessarily \u2014 strong earthquakes can follow, and JMA issues information about subsequent earthquake risk"],answer:3,explanation:"More strong shaking can follow a major earthquake. JMA operates dedicated information services about subsequent earthquake risk after large events. Keep a furniture-free space around you, keep shoes and your phone nearby, and stay tuned to official updates for days afterward.",source:{name:"Japan Meteorological Agency \u2014 Earthquakes and Tsunamis",url:"https://www.jma.go.jp/jma/en/Activities/earthquake.html"},difficulty:3,image:{key:"week-after",caption:"After a large earthquake, an equally large one remains possible for about a week \u2014 and large ones are most common in the first two or three days (JMA)"}},{id:"sur-006",category:"Survival",emoji:"\ud83d\ude89",title:"Crowded station, trains stopped",scenario:"Hours after a strong quake, trains in the region are still stopped, and the station is crowded.",question:"Why do trains stay stopped, and what should you do?",choices:["Trains are held until safety is confirmed \u2014 follow station staff and stay somewhere safe until service resumes","The drivers have gone home \u2014 find a taxi immediately","Trains are being kept for residents only","Walking along the tracks is the officially recommended alternative"],answer:0,explanation:"After shaking of around seismic intensity 4 or higher, railways in Japan may halt trains to confirm safety, and each operator decides when they run again. The stop is deliberate, not a breakdown. Crowding onto roads or tracks creates danger \u2014 follow station staff, and it's often wiser to wait somewhere safe than to force a long walk.",source:{name:"JMA Seismic Intensity Scale (explanation tables)",url:"https://www.jma.go.jp/jma/en/Activities/inttable.html"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"walk-home-2011",caption:"Akasaka, 17:19 on 11 March 2011: the road solid with cars, the sidewalks solid with people walking home. Photo: Hikosaemon (CC BY 2.0)"},infoLink:{name:"Tokyo Intercultural Portal Site \u2014 In the Event of an Earthquake (Tokyo Metropolitan Foundation TSUNAGARI)",url:"https://tabunka.tokyo-tsunagari.or.jp/english/disaster/howto/event_of_earthquake.html"}},{id:"sur-007",category:"Survival",emoji:"\ud83d\udd25",title:"Gas stove won't light next day",scenario:"After yesterday's strong earthquake, the gas stove in your apartment-hotel won't light at all.",question:"What's the most likely reason?",choices:["The earthquake destroyed the city's entire gas network","The gas meter's safety device tripped automatically during the strong shaking, cutting the supply","Gas is rationed to residents after earthquakes","The stove is broken and needs replacing"],answer:1,explanation:"Japanese gas meters have safety devices that trip at around seismic intensity 5 Lower or higher, automatically stopping the gas supply to prevent fires. Once you are sure there is no smell of gas, meters can usually be reset: turn off every gas appliance first, then follow the instructions on the unit \u2014 and ask staff for help rather than forcing it. After a very strong quake, the gas company may also have cut supply to the whole district; then resetting does nothing until it returns.",source:{name:"JMA Seismic Intensity Scale (explanation tables)",url:"https://www.jma.go.jp/jma/en/Activities/inttable.html"},difficulty:3,image:{key:"gas-meter",caption:"The gas meter's safety device: after strong shaking the red lamp blinks and the gas is cut off. Turn off every gas appliance, press the reset button, let go when the lamp lights, and wait three minutes before using gas"},infoLink:{name:"Tokyo Gas Network \u2014 resetting the gas meter (Japanese page)",url:"https://www.tokyo-gas.co.jp/network/meter/reset/meter_ippan/index.html"}},{id:"sur-008",category:"Survival",emoji:"\ud83e\udd1d",title:"Arriving at a crowded shelter",scenario:"You've arrived at an evacuation shelter in a school gym. It's crowded and you don't speak Japanese.",question:"What's the right way to settle in?",choices:["Claim the largest space you can find near the entrance","Avoid registering so you don't cause paperwork","Follow the staff's instructions, register if asked, and stay until the disaster risk passes","Leave immediately \u2014 shelters are not for foreigners"],answer:2,explanation:"Designated Evacuation Shelters are set up by the local government and run partly by the evacuees themselves. When you arrive, give your name and contact details at reception, ask the staff where to sleep, and check the shelter's rules. Some shelters also hand out meals and water to people who are not staying there. Gestures and translation apps go a long way.",source:{name:"Tokyo Intercultural Portal Site \u2014 About Evacuation Center (Tokyo Metropolitan Foundation TSUNAGARI)",url:"https://tabunka.tokyo-tsunagari.or.jp/english/disaster/howto/shelter.html"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"shelter-arrival",caption:"At reception give your name and contact details, ask staff where to sleep, check the rules \u2014 and remember that evacuees help run the place"},infoLink:{name:"Safety tips for travelers (Japan Tourism Agency / JNTO)",url:"https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/"}},{id:"sur-009",category:"Survival",emoji:"\ud83d\udec2",title:"Lost passport, canceled flight",scenario:"In a major disaster you've lost your passport, your flight is canceled, and you're not sure how to get home.",question:"Besides Japanese authorities, who should you contact?",choices:["Your country's embassy or consulate in Japan","The airline's frequent flyer desk","A travel blogger you follow","The local police station \u2014 they reissue passports for tourists"],answer:0,explanation:"If you lose your passport, or in an emergency or natural disaster, contact your embassy or consulate \u2014 a replacement passport has to come from your embassy. Note its location and contact details before you arrive; the link below shows how to find it. Police boxes take lost-property reports, but they cannot issue a passport.",source:{name:"Travel Japan \u2014 Staying Safe in Japan (JNTO)",url:"https://www.japan.travel/en/plan/emergencies/"},difficulty:1,image:{key:"passport-lost",caption:"Lost your passport? The report goes to a k\u014dban, but only your own embassy or consulate can replace it"},resources:["emergency-numbers"],infoLink:{name:"Travel Japan \u2014 Embassies (JNTO)",url:"https://www.japan.travel/en/plan/embassies/"}},{id:"sur-010",category:"Survival",emoji:"\ud83d\udeb6",title:"Crowds walking home in Tokyo",scenario:"A major earthquake hits Tokyo in the late afternoon. Trains have stopped, and your hotel is 15 km away. Crowds are starting to walk home along the big avenues.",question:"Should you join them?",choices:["Yes \u2014 following the locals is always the safest bet","Yes \u2014 better to walk now, before it gets dark","No \u2014 stay put somewhere safe and wait for the confusion to subside before moving","No \u2014 wave down a taxi; driving will be faster than walking"],answer:2,explanation:"Tokyo's official guidance asks people NOT to all start moving at once after a major quake: millions walking at the same time creates dangerously packed streets and blocks emergency vehicles. Roads jam too, so a taxi isn't the answer either. Confirm you're safe where you are, gather information, and move only once things have calmed down \u2014 Tokyo asks people to wait about three days, while rescue comes first. When you do set off, the yellow \u707d\u5bb3\u6642\u5e30\u5b85\u652f\u63f4\u30b9\u30c6\u30fc\u30b7\u30e7\u30f3 sticker is what to look for: convenience stores, gas stations and family restaurants along the main roads that offer tap water, a toilet, road information and somewhere to rest \u2014 in principle from the fourth day.",source:{name:"Disaster Preparedness Tokyo (Tokyo Metropolitan Government)",url:"https://www.bousai.metro.tokyo.lg.jp/_res/projects/default_project/_page_/001/029/136/tb2023_e_00.pdf"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"kitaku-station",caption:"The \u707d\u5bb3\u6642\u5e30\u5b85\u652f\u63f4\u30b9\u30c6\u30fc\u30b7\u30e7\u30f3 sticker: shops and gas stations along the main roads that give people walking home water, a toilet and information"}},{id:"sur-011",category:"Survival",emoji:"\ud83c\udfec",title:"No bed for the night in Tokyo",scenario:"After a big Tokyo earthquake you can't reach your hotel, trains are stopped, and evening is coming. You have nowhere to stay.",question:"Where can you go?",choices:["A temporary shelter for stranded people \u2014 they accept travelers and stock food and blankets","Nowhere \u2014 shelters are for residents, not foreign tourists","The airport \u2014 flights will still be leaving as usual","A karaoke box \u2014 by law they must host evacuees for free"],answer:0,explanation:"Tokyo opens temporary shelters specifically for people stranded away from home \u2014 including tourists with nowhere to go. They are stocked with food, blankets and other supplies for about three days. Opening information is published through the city's disaster channels, and station or hotel staff can point you to one. (And no, karaoke boxes have no such law \u2014 though many businesses do help voluntarily.)",source:{name:"Disaster Preparedness Tokyo (Tokyo Metropolitan Government)",url:"https://www.bousai.metro.tokyo.lg.jp/_res/projects/default_project/_page_/001/029/136/tb2023_e_00.pdf"},difficulty:2},{id:"sur-012",category:"Survival",emoji:"\ud83d\ude99",title:"Trapped in a flooded underpass",scenario:"Driving a rental car in heavy rain, you enter an underpass \u2014 and suddenly the car is in deep water and stalls. The doors won't open.",question:"How do you get out?",choices:["Throw all your strength at the door right away and force it open","Unbuckle first, then get out through a SIDE window \u2014 open it, or break it with an escape hammer","Phone 119 and wait calmly \u2014 rescue will arrive within minutes","Break the windshield \u2014 it's the biggest opening"],answer:1,explanation:"Outside water pressure can hold the doors shut, and underpasses flood fast in heavy rain. First, unbuckle. If the window is still above the water, open it and climb out onto the roof \u2014 power windows may keep working for a short while. If it won't open, break a SIDE window with a pointed escape hammer: the windshield is laminated and will not break even with one, and some cars use laminated side glass too. JAF tried things you might have in the car \u2014 a headrest, a phone, a car key, an umbrella \u2014 and none of them broke the glass; every one of the 51 escape hammers MLIT tested did. Keep a purpose-made one within reach when you drive. If nothing works, don't panic: as water comes in, the difference from the level outside shrinks and the pressure on the door eases. Then take a deep breath and push the door open. Best of all, never drive into a flooded underpass.",source:{name:"MLIT \u2014 carry an escape hammer: how to use and how to choose (Japanese page)",url:"https://renrakuda.mlit.go.jp/renrakuda/carsafety040.html"},infoLink:{name:"JAF user test \u2014 what breaks a car window under water (Japanese page)",url:"https://jaf.or.jp/common/safety-drive/car-learning/user-test/disaster/window"},difficulty:3,image:{key:"underpass",caption:"An underpass with flood-depth marks on the walls \u2014 1.0 m, 0.5 m, 0.3 m. These dips fill quickly in heavy rain",withQuestion:!0},explanationImage:{key:"escape-hammer",caption:"An escape hammer: the point breaks a side window, the blade cuts a seat belt. Keep it where you can reach it"}},{id:"sur-013",category:"Survival",emoji:"\ud83d\udd73\ufe0f",title:"Flooded street to your hotel",scenario:"A sudden downpour has flooded the street between you and your hotel. It seems to be below your knees, and it looks as if you could wade across.",question:"Can you walk through it?",choices:["Yes \u2014 floodwater in cities is shallow and safe to walk through","Yes \u2014 but take off your shoes first so they don't get soaked","Yes \u2014 walk along the edge of the road, where it looks shallower","Avoid it if you can; if you must cross, probe every step with an umbrella or a long stick \u2014 manhole and gutter covers may have come off"],answer:3,explanation:"Muddy floodwater hides its dangers: manhole and gutter covers can lift off, leaving invisible holes at your feet \u2014 and the road edge is exactly where the gutters run. Even knee-deep water makes walking difficult, and bare feet are an injury waiting to happen \u2014 keep your sneakers on. If you can stay somewhere safe or move to an upper floor, do that. If you truly must cross, probe every step with an umbrella or a long stick.",source:{name:"Disaster Preparedness Tokyo (Tokyo Metropolitan Government)",url:"https://www.bousai.metro.tokyo.lg.jp/_res/projects/default_project/_page_/001/029/136/tb2023_e_00.pdf"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"flooded-road",caption:"A city street under muddy floodwater in a downpour",withQuestion:!0},infoLink:{name:"MLIT \u2014 flood depth and how to evacuate (Japanese page)",url:"https://city.river.go.jp/kawabou/reference/index05.html"}},{id:"act-019",category:"Action",emoji:"\u2b06\ufe0f",title:"Underground mall starts to flood",scenario:"You're in an underground shopping mall connected to the station when an Emergency Warning for heavy rain is issued. Water has started running down the stairs from the street.",question:"What should you do?",choices:["Stay where you are \u2014 underground is sheltered from the storm","Move deeper inside, away from the stairs the water is coming down","Get above ground and up to a higher floor, while the water is still shallow","Wait for staff to bring sandbags to the entrances"],answer:2,explanation:"Tokyo's guidance for foreign residents lists flooded subways and underpasses among the main hazards of heavy rain, and says roads can overflow and become like rivers. It tells people in at-risk areas to evacuate by Alert Level 4, and at Level 5, which corresponds to a heavy rain Emergency Warning, to shelter wherever is safest, such as an upper floor. Underground there are no upper floors, so the way to go is up and out. The deeper the water pouring down the stairs, the harder they are to climb, so move while it is still shallow.",source:{name:"Tokyo Intercultural Portal Site (Tokyo Metropolitan Foundation TSUNAGARI)",url:"https://tabunka.tokyo-tsunagari.or.jp/english/disaster/howto/typhoon.html"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"exit-sign",caption:"Green \u975e\u5e38\u53e3 (EXIT) signs mark the way out of stations and underground malls"}},{id:"inf-015",category:"Information",emoji:"\ud83c\udf9a\ufe0f",title:"Alert Level 4 for heavy rain",scenario:"Your phone buzzes with a heavy-rain alert for the area where you're staying. It says Alert Level 4.",question:"What does Level 4 mean for you?",choices:["It's a heads-up \u2014 get your bag ready and wait for Level 5 before moving","Everyone in a risk area evacuates now; finish while it's still Level 4","A disaster has already happened somewhere nearby","It is aimed at residents \u2014 hotel guests are covered by the hotel"],answer:1,explanation:"For heavy rain, flooding, landslides and storm surge, Japan's weather warnings line up with a five-level alert scale. At Level 3, people who need more time should start moving. At Level 4, JMA says everyone in a dangerous place should evacuate, and that you should finish evacuating while Level 4 is still in effect. Level 5 does not mean 'now it's serious' \u2014 it means a disaster is already happening or is imminent. The government warns against waiting for Level 5, because your town might not issue the alert in time. Knowing you must leave is only half the plan: you also need to know where to go. Local towns designate evacuation sites. You can find hazard maps and shelters in English on the MLIT disaster prevention portal. At a hotel, ask the front desk for the nearest one.",source:{name:"Japan Meteorological Agency \u2014 Emergency Warning System",url:"https://www.jma.go.jp/jma/en/Emergency_Warning/ew_index.html"},infoLink:{name:"Hazard maps and evacuation sites (MLIT Disaster Prevention Portal)",url:"https://www.mlit.go.jp/river/bousai/bousai-portal/en/prepare01/index.html"},difficulty:2,image:{key:"alert-levels",caption:"Alert levels 1\u20135 for heavy rain and floods: at Level 4 everyone in a risk area evacuates; Level 5 means a disaster is under way or imminent"}},{id:"act-020",category:"Action",emoji:"\ud83d\uded1",title:"Rain to halt trains tomorrow",scenario:"Heavy rain is forecast for tomorrow. The railway announces that the lines you planned to use will stop running from the morning.",question:"What should you do?",choices:["Get to the station early tomorrow and wait \u2014 service usually returns within an hour or two","Treat tomorrow's travel as impossible: change your plans tonight and make sure you have a place to stay","Ignore it \u2014 announced suspensions get called off once the storm weakens","Switch to a highway bus, since roads keep running in heavy rain"],answer:1,explanation:"This is keikaku-unkyu, a planned mass transit suspension: rather than let trains halt mid-route, operators announce ahead of time that services will stop. In heavy rain and typhoons, trains and buses may run less frequently or be suspended entirely, and roads flood as well, so a bus is no guarantee either. The easy mistake is to read the announcement as a delay and camp at the station. Use the warning while you still have it: secure a room, buy food and water, and plan to stay put.",image:{key:"planned-suspension",caption:"The notice comes in advance. The evening in between is the time you have"},source:{name:"Tokyo Intercultural Portal Site (Tokyo Metropolitan Foundation TSUNAGARI)",url:"https://tabunka.tokyo-tsunagari.or.jp/english/disaster/howto/typhoon.html"},difficulty:2}]},1123,[]);
__d(function(e,a,t,o,n,i,s){n.exports=[{id:"fact-act-001",text:"Why is your teacher a catfish? In Japanese folklore, earthquakes were blamed on \u014cnamazu \u2014 a giant catfish living beneath the islands. When it thrashes, the ground shakes.",image:{key:"namazu-apology",caption:"A namazu-e of 1855: catfish in kimono bow low before a god, with townspeople looking on"}},{id:"fact-act-002",text:"Japan's five-story wooden pagodas have stood through centuries of earthquakes \u2014 records show almost none have ever toppled during one. The reason is still debated: explanations include flexible joinery that sways and absorbs the shock instead of fighting it, and the central pillar running up the middle. Ordinary buildings are another matter, though: Japanese houses built before 1981 collapse far more often than ones built to today's rules.",image:{key:"pagoda",caption:"A five-story pagoda: five roofs, and under each of them the bracket work that carries the weight"}},{id:"fact-act-003",text:"Japan's real earthquake 'prediction' is physics, not fish: the Earthquake Early Warning system detects the fast P-waves with seismometers near the epicenter and broadcasts alerts that can arrive seconds before the stronger shaking does. That's why phones sometimes ring just before the strong shaking starts.",image:{key:"ps-waves",caption:"The P wave runs at about 7 km a second and the S wave at about 4 \u2014 at 100 km that is 14 seconds against 25"}},{id:"fact-act-004",text:"For decades, Japanese drills taught 'first, turn off the stove!' Modern guidance flipped the order: protect yourself first. Why? Today's gas meters shut themselves off \u2014 and grabbing a pot of boiling oil mid-shake is how people fall or get scalded, while the fire risk from a stove in use is now low."},{id:"fact-act-005",text:"Check your Japanese hotel room when you arrive. Many hotels keep a flashlight by the bed or in the closet for a blackout \u2014 after a quake, or in a fire.",image:{key:"hotel-lamp",caption:"A wall-mounted \u5e38\u5099\u706f: pull it out of the bracket and it lights. Where it lives varies by hotel \u2014 by the bed, in the closet, or on the wall like this. Photo: nagi usano (CC BY 4.0)"}},{id:"fact-act-006",text:"How do Japanese museums keep priceless ceramics standing through earthquakes? Anti-vibration gel pads and hidden wires are among the tools. Shops and homes use similar quake gel under TVs and vases \u2014 a few hundred yen at most home centers.",image:{key:"quake-gel",caption:"Quake gel pads, to go under furniture and appliances \u2014 a few hundred yen at most home centers"}},{id:"fact-act-007",text:"Many newer Japanese elevators sense a quake, stop at the nearest floor, and open their doors automatically; some feel the fast P-wave and stop before the strong shaking arrives. Past quakes left people trapped between floors, and since 2009 such a device has been required on newly installed elevators.",image:{key:"elevator-stops",caption:"Sensing a quake, the elevator stops at the nearest floor and opens its doors \u2014 required on cars installed since 28 Sep 2009"}},{id:"fact-act-008",text:"The catfish is still Japan's unofficial earthquake mascot today \u2014 you'll find it on the roadside boards that mark routes set aside for emergency vehicles after a big quake, and on disaster-preparedness materials across the country.",image:{key:"emergency-road-sign",caption:"The catfish on an Emergency Road sign: after a big quake these roads are kept clear for emergency vehicles"}},{id:"fact-act-009",text:"On the back of your hotel room door in Japan you'll often find an evacuation route map. Ten seconds of looking tonight makes it easier to find the way out if the alarm sounds at 2 a.m.",image:{key:"evac-route-map",caption:"An evacuation route map: the rooms in a row, the way out marked, and the green exit sign at the stairs"}},{id:"fact-act-010",text:"The Shinkansen has carried billions of passengers since 1964. Its earthquake sensors can start braking before you feel anything. In a quake, a sudden stop is usually the system working, not failing.",image:{key:"shinkansen-brake",caption:"When a seismometer picks up the P wave, power to the line is cut and the train brakes to a stop. In a quake, a sudden stop is usually the system working (example: JR East)"}},{id:"fact-act-011",text:"After the 2011 earthquake, navigation data from cars on the road was turned, from the very next day, into maps of which roads had actually been driven \u2014 helping rescuers and evacuees find passable routes. Your rental car may be quietly contributing today.",image:{key:"probe-roads",caption:"Tracks from car navigation systems were pooled into a map of the roads that were passable, published the morning after the 2011 quake (Honda)"}},{id:"fact-act-012",text:"The grand curtain in a Japanese theater is the one you see. A large stage carries a second one you do not \u2014 a fire curtain that can seal the stage off in an emergency. Theater staff drill for quakes and fires; trust their choreography.",image:{key:"theatre-curtain",caption:"What you see here is the grand curtain. The fire curtain is not in the picture; it comes down only in an emergency"}},{id:"fact-act-013",text:"At four in the afternoon in 1854, a great earthquake struck the Kii Peninsula. After the first wave had washed through his village and drawn back, soy-sauce merchant Hamaguchi Gory\u014d was certain that people were lost in the dark. He set fire to stacks of rice sheaves to show the way to high ground. Retold as the story 'Inamura-no-hi' \u2014 where a village headman burns his harvest before the wave arrives \u2014 it is why November 5 is now World Tsunami Awareness Day.",image:{key:"hamaguchi-goryo",caption:"Hamaguchi Gory\u014d in later life \u2014 the seawall he paid for after 1854 still stands in his village"}},{id:"fact-act-014",text:"Shindo 7, the maximum level on Japan's intensity scale, has been recorded only eight times in observation history \u2014 including Kobe 1995, T\u014dhoku 2011, Noto 2024 and Kumamoto 2026. The scale simply has nothing above it.",image:{key:"shindo7-times",caption:"Every Shindo 7 on record, 1995 to 2026 \u2014 three of them in Kumamoto: twice in 2016 and again in 2026"}},{id:"fact-act-015",text:"Why does Japan have thousands of hot springs? The same tectonic forces that cause earthquakes heat the water. Onsen are the planet's apology gift \u2014 the upside of living on the Ring of Fire.",image:{key:"plates-japan",caption:"Four plates meet around Japan. The two ocean plates slide under the land a few centimeters a year (schematic, after JMA)"}},{id:"fact-act-016",text:"Tokyo Skytree's earthquake damping is named after a 1,300-year-old idea: the central pillar (shinbashira) of Japan's ancient pagodas, which moves independently of the tower around it. Its engineers say a modern damping design ended up resembling the pagoda, so they borrowed the name in homage.",image:{key:"shinbashira",caption:"Skytree's central cylinder stands apart from the steel frame, so its top can work as a counterweight"}},{id:"fact-act-017",text:"Tokyo's Ginza Line opened in 1927, the first subway in Asia. It has run through fires, war and countless earthquakes, and it's still carrying passengers today. Subway tunnels move with the ground around them and have mostly come through earthquakes well \u2014 though not always: in Kobe in 1995 the moving ground sheared the columns of Daikai Station, built before underground structures were designed for quakes."},{id:"fact-act-018",text:"Osaka's Umeda and Tokyo's Yaesu are among Japan's largest underground shopping streets. In a quake there, wait for the emergency lighting and follow the staff out."},{id:"fact-bas-001",text:"'Tsunami' (\u6d25\u6ce2 \u2014 'harbor wave') has been an English word since 1896, the year of the Meiji Sanriku tsunami.",image:{key:"tsunami-word",caption:"\u6d25 is a harbor and \u6ce2 a wave \u2014 the Japanese word reached English in 1896, the year of the Meiji Sanriku earthquake"}},{id:"fact-bas-002",text:"News reports still often say 'Richter scale', but Japan does not use it. JMA gives a quake's size in its own magnitude, and how hard each place shook on the Shindo intensity scale. For a traveler, the Shindo for the town you are in is usually the more useful number."},{id:"fact-bas-003",text:"Until 1996, Shindo intensity was judged by trained officials' own senses \u2014 how the room felt, what fell over. Today a nationwide network of seismometers does it automatically in seconds \u2014 from human body to silicon. In October 1996, a second revision split 5 and 6 into Lower and Upper, taking the scale from eight steps to ten.",image:{key:"shindo-by-hand",caption:"Until March 1996 Shindo was estimated from what people felt and saw; from April 1996 seismometers report it automatically. That October a second revision split 5 and 6 into Lower and Upper (JMA)"}},{id:"fact-bas-004",text:"Japan's tsunami warning service began in 1952. In the 1950s and '60s, when staff worked it out by hand, a warning typically took 10 to 20 minutes; today it takes about 3. Near the coast, those minutes are lives.",image:{key:"warning-minutes",caption:"In the 1950s and '60s a tsunami warning typically took 10 to 20 minutes; today it takes about 3"}},{id:"fact-bas-005",text:"Japan moors GPS wave gauges offshore. They feel a tsunami pass beneath them before it reaches the coast \u2014 an early tripwire that has confirmed real waves while people onshore were still evacuating.",image:{key:"gps-wave-gauge",caption:"Eighteen of these were moored around Japan as of 2019; the data also feeds the wave forecasts for ports"}},{id:"fact-bas-006",text:"1981 is a dividing line for Japanese buildings: the 'shin-taishin' earthquake-resistance code. In the 1995 Kobe quake, damage was concentrated in buildings built to the older rules. The line is still alive today \u2014 'new-taishin or old?' is a standard checkbox when buying an older home, and municipal subsidies for seismic inspections target pre-1981 buildings.",image:{key:"shin-taishin",caption:"1981 divides the building code, and the damage in 1995 fell mostly on what came before it"}},{id:"fact-bas-007",text:"People long believed catfish behave strangely before earthquakes, and Japanese researchers seriously studied this in the 20th century. No reliable link was ever proven \u2014 so trust official warnings, not fish (sorry!)."},{id:"fact-bas-008",text:"At Kashima Shrine in Ibaraki, a sacred stone called the kaname-ishi is said to pin down the giant catfish's head and keep earthquakes in check. Only its tip shows above ground \u2014 legend says no one has ever dug deep enough to reach its bottom.",image:{key:"kaname-ishi",caption:"A namazu-e of the kaname-ishi \u2014 the stone marked \u8981\u77f3 in red \u2014 with a crowd trying to shift it"}},{id:"fact-com-001",text:"Japan's fire-and-ambulance number began as 112, chosen in 1926 when telephone exchanges went automatic. Misconnections were so frequent that an investigation traced them partly to the exchange equipment itself, so in 1927 the last digit became 9, a digit no exchange code used: 119. The story that the long pull of the 9 slows a panicked finger is often told, but the records point to the exchange.",image:{key:"dial-119",caption:"On a rotary dial the 1 sits by the finger stop and the 9 far from it. The change to 119 was made for the exchange: no exchange code used a 9"}},{id:"fact-com-002",text:"Japan has thousands of k\u014dban \u2014 small neighborhood police boxes whose officers cover the streets around them. For a stolen bag, the nearest k\u014dban is where your report begins.",image:{key:"koban",caption:"A k\u014dban: the red lamp and the KOBAN sign mark a neighborhood police post"}},{id:"fact-com-003",text:"The 1995 Kobe earthquake left many foreign residents lost in a flood of complex announcements. In response, Japan developed 'yasashii nihongo' \u2014 Easy Japanese: simplified phrasing now used in disaster broadcasts, signage and city offices nationwide.",image:{key:"easy-japanese",caption:"Easy Japanese: a hard word, then the same thing in plain words, with the reading over each kanji (Immigration Services Agency's list, English added)"}},{id:"fact-com-004",text:"A handy way to remember 171: in Japanese number wordplay, 1-7-1 can be read 'i-na-i' \u2014 'not here.' It fits what the number is for: leaving word when you can't be reached. Number puns like this are common in Japan.",image:{key:"dial-171-inai",caption:"1 is i, 7 is na, 1 is i: 171 reads i-na-i, Japanese for 'not here' \u2014 the number for leaving word with someone who is not beside you"}},{id:"fact-com-005",text:"You can practice the 171 disaster message line for real: it opens for trial use on the 1st and 15th of every month, January 1\u20133, August 30\u2013September 5 (Disaster Prevention Week) and January 15\u201321. Two minutes of practice on a trial day means less fumbling in a real disaster."},{id:"fact-com-006",text:"When a large disaster cuts the network, Japan's Wi-Fi operators open one free network across the affected area: 00000JAPAN \u2014 five zeros, pronounced 'five-zero Japan'. No password, no sign-up; pick it from the list and you are online. Security is traded for convenience \u2014 the traffic is not encrypted, so the guidelines ask you to keep it to checking in and reading the news.",image:{key:"wifi-00000japan",caption:"The disaster Wi-Fi 00000JAPAN shows no lock icon in the list: no password needed, but the traffic is not encrypted (WiBiz guideline)"}},{id:"fact-com-007",text:"118 reaches the Japan Coast Guard \u2014 one number for anything at sea, from sinking boats to drifting divers. The Coast Guard got its own three-digit number, like 110 and 119, in 2000."},{id:"fact-com-008",text:"Public ambulances in Japan are free \u2014 so what helps keep them available for real emergencies? One tool is #7119: a hotline in Tokyo and many regions where medical staff assess your symptoms and tell you whether to call 119 now or simply see a doctor tomorrow. The hours differ by region; in Tokyo it runs around the clock. The ambulance itself is still free, but since December 2024 large hospitals in Ibaraki Prefecture have charged up to \xa513,200 when they judge the call was not urgent. Unsure? Call #7119. Urgent? Call 119 without hesitating. #7119 is in this app's Emergency tab, too.",image:{key:"dial-7119-map",caption:"Red: #7119 covers the whole prefecture. Pink: some municipalities only. White: not there yet. The map shows the plan for the end of March 2026. Source: FDMA, 2025 White Paper on Fire Service, special feature figure 4-4, enlarged"}},{id:"fact-com-009",text:"Edo-era Tokyo had celebrity firefighters \u2014 the hikeshi, famous for their tattoos, banner standards (matoi) and turf pride. They put fires out by pulling houses down: their hand pumps (ry\u016bdosui) could not throw water far, so stopping a blaze in a city of wood and paper meant tearing out everything in its path. Their direct heirs today are Tokyo's volunteer fire corps, the sh\u014db\u014ddan; the professional Tokyo Fire Department grew from a separate force founded in 1880.",image:{key:"hikeshi",caption:"An Edo firefighter in an 1860 woodblock print, with lanterns behind him"}},{id:"fact-com-010",text:"J-ALERT is Japan's national instant warning system: the government sends alerts by satellite and landline to every town hall, which sets off its outdoor loudspeakers automatically, and through the mobile carriers to phones \u2014 all within seconds. The Safety tips app gives you official warnings in English.",image:{key:"disaster-radio",caption:"An outdoor loudspeaker of the town's disaster radio. J-ALERT sets it off automatically; if you can't follow the announcement, check your phone and the app"}},{id:"fact-com-011",text:"LINE \u2014 a messaging app with more than 100 million monthly users in Japan \u2014 was born from the 2011 earthquake, built in under two months so families could reach each other when calls failed. Even its 'read' receipt exists so a single glance can say 'I'm alive.'"},{id:"fact-com-012",text:"Japan's payphones are protected by law: universal-service rules still require one within roughly every one-kilometer square in cities (one per two-kilometer square elsewhere). Most draw power through the phone line, so in a blackout you can usually still call with coins, though not with a phone card. Emergency calls are free.",image:{key:"payphone-density",caption:"The payphones kept for universal service are placed according to a national standard: roughly one per 1 km square in built-up areas, one per 2 km square elsewhere (MIC)"}},{id:"fact-inf-001",text:"Emergency alerts don't dial millions of numbers \u2014 they use cell broadcast: one radio message received by every phone in the target area at once. That's why alerts get through even when the network is jammed with calls."},{id:"fact-inf-002",text:"An earthquake warning is a race: alert signals travel at the speed of light, while damaging S-waves crawl along at roughly 4 km per second. The farther you are from the epicenter, the more seconds the light wins. Directly on top of it, physics offers no head start.",image:{key:"eew-race",caption:"The bar is how long the S wave takes to reach you; the warning goes out a few seconds after the quake, so your head start is a little shorter"}},{id:"fact-inf-003",text:"What can ten seconds do? Trains brake, elevators stop at the nearest floor, factory lines halt \u2014 and surgeons are said to lift their scalpels away from the patient.",image:{key:"ten-seconds",caption:"Ten seconds of warning: trains brake, elevators stop at the nearest floor, factory lines stop (JMA) \u2014 and a surgeon lifts the scalpel away from the patient, they say"}},{id:"fact-inf-004",text:"The Emergency Warning tier (tokubetsu keih\u014d) was created in 2013, applying lessons from the 2011 disaster \u2014 the old top level didn't sound alarming enough for once-in-decades catastrophes. If you ever see one and it is not marked \u8a13\u7df4 (drill), treat it as real and act at once."},{id:"fact-inf-005",text:"Japanese media coined a vivid name for sudden urban downpours: 'guerrilla rainstorms' (gerira g\u014du) \u2014 rain that ambushes one neighborhood with little warning. The word stuck because the experience is exactly that.",image:{key:"ohashi-yudachi",caption:"Hiroshige, Sudden Shower over \u014chashi Bridge (1857): caught by the downpour, people on the bridge hunch over and hurry on"}},{id:"fact-inf-006",text:"For tsunami forecasts, Japan's coastline is divided into 66 regions, each warned separately with its own estimated wave height. That colored map on TV shows all 66 at once.",image:{key:"tsunami-66",caption:"The coast is cut into 66 tsunami forecast regions, each with its own warning and its own estimated height (the colors are an example)"}},{id:"fact-inf-007",text:"Earthquake Early Warnings come in two grades: the public warning sent to phones, and a technical 'forecast' feed that railways and factories act on for smaller quakes. Yours is the one your phone sounds; the trains are reading the quiet one.",image:{key:"eew-grades",caption:"Warning is the one your phone sounds; Forecast goes to rail and factories from smaller quakes"}},{id:"fact-inf-008",text:"In 1960, an earthquake in Chile sent a tsunami across the whole Pacific \u2014 it reached Japan about a day later, with no shaking felt here at all. That disaster is a major reason Pacific nations now share tsunami warnings with each other."},{id:"fact-inf-009",text:"That two-tone TV chime was created by Tohru Ifukube \u2014 nephew of Akira Ifukube, composer of Godzilla's theme. A Godzilla motif was actually considered for the alert and rejected as too frightening; the chime is based on his uncle's symphony instead."},{id:"fact-inf-010",text:"Every year, Osaka's 8.8 Million Person Drill sends a practice emergency alert about a Major Tsunami Warning to phones across the prefecture. It began in 2012, a lesson from 2011, when some people died without evacuating even though a tsunami warning was out. Depending on your settings it sounds even on silent, but it is not the Earthquake Early Warning tone. Its subject reads \u8a13\u7df4\u901a\u5831DRILL, and the message also contains \u8a13\u7df4 (kunren, 'drill') and DRILL. If you see them, breathe out.",image:{key:"drill-alert",caption:"A sample of Osaka's drill alert: \u8a13\u7df4 and DRILL appear in both the subject and the message (from Osaka Prefecture's message lists)"}},{id:"fact-inf-011",text:"NHK WORLD-JAPAN broadcasts disaster coverage in English \u2014 streaming free online and in its app, with live emergency programming when big quakes hit. Bookmark it next to the Safety tips app, and when the Japanese broadcasts are hard to follow, you have somewhere to check in English."},{id:"fact-inf-012",text:"After the great Ansei Edo earthquake of 1855, woodblock prints called namazu-e ('catfish pictures') became wildly popular in Edo (today's Tokyo). They showed the giant catfish being scolded, punished, or apologizing for the quake.",image:{key:"namazu-beaten",caption:"A namazu-e of 1855: people beat the giant catfish with sticks and haul it by its whiskers. Adapted from an image courtesy of the University of Tokyo General Library"}},{id:"fact-inf-013",text:"Japan's sudden downpours aren't just a feeling. JMA counts roughly 340 bursts a year of 50 mm or more in a single hour from 2016 to 2025, up from about 226 a year from 1976 to 1985. That is about 1.5 times as many, scaled to a network of about 1,300 gauges. Downpours this heavy really are more common than they used to be.",image:{key:"rain-bursts",caption:"Downpours of 50 mm or more in one hour: about 226 a year in 1976\u201385, about 340 in 2016\u201325"}},{id:"fact-inf-014",text:"Deep under Saitama, just north of Tokyo, sits one of the world's largest underground flood channels: a 6.3-km tunnel feeding a cathedral-like tank where 59 pillars stand 18 meters tall \u2014 fans call it the 'Underground Temple.' It swallows overflowing rivers during torrential rain, and on dry days you can tour it.",image:{key:"floodway",caption:"On a dry day you can walk the floor of the tank; in a downpour the overflowing river fills it"}},{id:"fact-sur-001",text:"Peek into an elementary school classroom around Tokyo: the seat cushions are often b\u014dsai zukin \u2014 padded disaster hoods. Kids sit on them all year and wear them over their heads during earthquake drills and evacuations. Safety, disguised as furniture.",image:{key:"bousai-zukin",caption:"A b\u014dsai zukin, half unfolded: used as a seat cushion, it opens into a padded hood worn over the head"}},{id:"fact-sur-002",text:"'Tsunami stones' dot Japan's northeast coast \u2014 some 300 of them, most raised after the tsunamis of 1896 and 1933 \u2014 carved with warnings like 'Do not build your home below this point.' Aneyoshi's was raised after the 1933 tsunami, and in 2011 the hamlet, which had obeyed it, stayed above the waves.",image:{key:"tsunami-stone",caption:"The stone records two tsunamis that reached this spot in 1896 and 1933, and warns: however many years pass, be on your guard. Photo: GSI Natural Disaster Monuments (provided by Miyako City), enlarged"}},{id:"fact-sur-003",text:"The Sanriku coast has a hard-won saying: 'tsunami tendenko' \u2014 when a tsunami comes, run separately, each for your own life. In 2011, the pupils of two schools in Kamaishi's Unosumai district, who had trained on that principle, led their own evacuation uphill \u2014 and all of the roughly 570 at school that day survived."},{id:"fact-sur-004",text:"Japan gives everything a mascot \u2014 even disaster preparedness. When Tokyo mailed a survival handbook to every household in the city, it came fronted by B\u014dsai-kun, a cartoon rhinoceros ('sai', rhino, hides inside 'b\u014dsai'). If a rhino gets more people to open an emergency manual, the rhino has earned his keep."},{id:"fact-sur-005",text:"Japan retired an old assumption in 2016: in the Kumamoto earthquakes, a magnitude 7.3 shock followed the 'main' 6.5 about 28 hours later. Since then, the official warning after big quakes is that similar-scale earthquakes may follow for about a week.",image:{key:"kumamoto-2016",caption:"Kumamoto, 2016: an M7.3 struck about 28 hours after the M6.5, and both reached Shindo 7 (JMA)"}},{id:"fact-sur-006",text:"On March 11, 2011, Tokyo's trains stopped and huge crowds walked home through the night \u2014 some for six hours or more. That night rewrote the playbook: when everyone walks at once, the streets jam solid and ambulances and fire engines can't get through. Today's official advice: don't rush to walk home \u2014 stay somewhere safe first."},{id:"fact-sur-007",text:"The microcomputer gas meter watches for tremors and abnormal flow, and shuts off your gas by itself. It has been quietly preventing post-quake fires since the 1980s. Reset instructions are printed right on the meter."},{id:"fact-sur-008",text:"Japan's shelters were long notorious for rows of people sleeping straight on gym floors. The 2024 Noto earthquake forced a rewrite: national guidelines now call for cardboard beds and privacy partitions to go up when a shelter opens. Sleeping on cardboard sounds grim, but it keeps you warmer than lying directly on a cold floor.",image:{key:"cardboard-beds",caption:"Cardboard beds and partitions set up in a school gym. They lift you off the cold floor and give a little privacy"}},{id:"fact-sur-009",text:"More than half of all the embassies in Japan cluster in a single Tokyo ward: Minato City \u2014 which makes it more likely than not that yours is one of them. Save the address before you fly.",image:{key:"embassies-minato",caption:"81 of the 157 embassies in Japan are in Minato City \u2014 the first legations came here, and the state later gave out the grounds of former daimyo estates"}},{id:"fact-sur-010",text:"Tokyo has an ordinance for earthquake gridlock: companies are asked to keep employees at the office \u2014 with three days of stockpiled water, food and blankets \u2014 rather than send everyone into the streets at once. Rush hour is bad enough without a disaster.",image:{key:"tokyo-stockpile",caption:"Tokyo's ordinance asks employers to stock three days per employee; the city's handbook puts that at 9 liters of water, 9 meals and a blanket"}},{id:"fact-sur-011",text:"Some Japanese vending machines are 'disaster relief vendors': in a disaster, whoever holds the key turns it and the drinks come out free. Look for the disaster-support sticker.",image:{key:"relief-vendor",caption:"The \u707d\u5bb3\u6551\u63f4\u30d9\u30f3\u30c0\u30fc sticker: this machine carries a battery, so it can still hand out drinks when the power is down"}},{id:"fact-sur-012",text:"JAF, Japan's automobile federation, tested what can break a car's window when you are trapped underwater. Only purpose-made escape hammers got through the side glass: a headrest, a phone, a car key and an umbrella all failed, and even a hammer could not break the windshield. No law requires one, meaning a rental car may not have it \u2014 so get the window open early, and treat a flooded underpass as a road that doesn't exist.",image:{key:"flooded-car",caption:"A car stopped in floodwater on an ordinary street in heavy rain. As the water rises, its pressure makes the doors hard to push open"}},{id:"fact-sur-013",text:"Japan's manhole covers are local art: cities compete with ornate designs, and official collectible 'manhole cards' are issued for them, which fans hunt nationwide. In a flood, though, even the prettiest cover can lift away \u2014 one more reason to probe the water ahead of your feet.",image:{key:"manhole",caption:"Yokohama's cover: a hippo in the waves, and the city's name cast in katakana \u2014 the pavement tells you where you are"}},{id:"fact-act-019",text:"Look down at the entrance of a Japanese underground mall and you'll often spot slim metal channels set into the floor, with a stack of boards waiting nearby. They're flood barriers, slotted in by staff when heavy rain is coming. Spotting them going up is a useful signal \u2014 the safe direction from there is up and out."},{id:"fact-inf-015",text:"Level 4 used to carry two separate calls: an 'evacuation advisory' and, above it, an 'evacuation order'. People kept waiting for the stronger one. In 2021 the advisory was abolished and folded into a single order, because that extra rung made people hesitate. The lesson is now built into the ladder itself.",image:{key:"evac-order-2021",caption:"Level 4 had two calls until 2021, an advisory and an order; people waited for the stronger one, so now there is only one"}},{id:"fact-act-020",text:"Planned suspensions are fairly new. Japanese railways used to run until the weather stopped them \u2014 until JR West announced ahead of a 2014 typhoon that it would stop its local lines around Osaka, Kobe and Kyoto. It drew complaints at first, then became standard practice nationwide. Deciding in advance to stop spares passengers from being stranded mid-route.",image:{key:"suspended-board",caption:"\u904b\u4f11 Suspended means the whole run is off; \u4e00\u90e8\u904b\u4f11 means part of it, with the section named on the right"}}]},1124,[]);
